window.NEXUS_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"t":"Nexus Documentation","g":"Overview","u":"graph-nexus/index.html","x":"Nexus Documentation Nexus is a JavaScript SDK for network visualization and timelines. These guides cover installation, the chart, time bar and timeline components, styling, layouts and the graph analytics behind them. Overview # API Index API Signatures Installing Nexus # Installing Nexus Importing Nexus Nexus and TypeScript Upgrading Using Nexus with Angular Using Nexus with React Using Nexus with Rollup Using Nexus with Vue Using Nexus with Webpack Using Nexus with plain JavaScript Basics # Architecture Chart Basics Combos Events Basics Layout Basics Loading Data Map Mode Time Bar Features # 3D View Aggregate Links Chart Export Combos Concepts Fonts and Font Icons Graph Centrality Graph Engine Node and Combo Styling PDF Export Right-to-Left Text Self Links Time Zones Best Practices # Asynchronous JavaScript Coding and Testing Tips Cross-Origin Images (CORS) Image Formats Obfuscation Requirements Resizing Components Troubleshooting Third Party Integrations # Angular Reference Neo4j Tutorial React Reference Platforms and Dependencies Vue Reference WebGL About Nexus # Accessibility Alpha and Beta Features Deprecation Process Performance Security Timeline · Overview # API Reference "},{"t":"Overview","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#overview"},{"t":"Installing Nexus","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#installing-nexus"},{"t":"Basics","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#basics"},{"t":"Features","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#features"},{"t":"Best Practices","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#best-practices"},{"t":"Third Party Integrations","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#third-party-integrations"},{"t":"About Nexus","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#about-nexus"},{"t":"Timeline · Overview","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#timeline-overview"},{"t":"Timeline · Getting Started","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#timeline-getting-started"},{"t":"Timeline · Basics","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#timeline-basics"},{"t":"Timeline · Items","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#timeline-items"},{"t":"Timeline · Data and Time","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#timeline-data-and-time"},{"t":"Timeline · Interaction","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#timeline-interaction"},{"t":"Timeline · Integration","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#timeline-integration"},{"t":"Timeline · About","g":"Nexus Documentation","u":"graph-nexus/index.html#timeline-about"},{"t":"Nexus API Index","g":"Overview","u":"graph-nexus/api.html","x":"Nexus API Index Every Nexus API symbol referred to by these guides, grouped by object, with the guides that describe each one. This index records the symbols the guides reference and where they are explained. It is not a full signature reference: parameter lists and return types are documented in the guide pages linked beside each entry. GraphNexus # Entry point for creating components and global helpers. 16 referenced symbols GraphNexus.callbacks Described in API Signatures GraphNexus.components Described in API Signatures GraphNexus.coords Described in API Signatures GraphNexus.corsAdapter Described in API Signatures · Cross-Origin Images (CORS) GraphNexus.create Described in API Signatures · Chart Basics · Loading Data · Resizing Components · Using Nexus with plain JavaScript GraphNexus.createTimeline Described in API Signatures GraphNexus.dashedLineSupport Described in API Signatures GraphNexus.fullScreenCapable Described in API Signatures GraphNexus.getFontIcon Described in API Signatures · Fonts and Font Icons GraphNexus.getGraphEngine Described in API Signatures · Combos Concepts · Self Links GraphNexus.info Described in API Signatures · Troubleshooting GraphNexus.mode Descr"},{"t":"GraphNexus","g":"Nexus API Index","u":"graph-nexus/api.html#GraphNexus"},{"t":"Chart","g":"Nexus API Index","u":"graph-nexus/api.html#Chart"},{"t":"ChartOptions","g":"Nexus API Index","u":"graph-nexus/api.html#ChartOptions"},{"t":"ChartEvents","g":"Nexus API Index","u":"graph-nexus/api.html#ChartEvents"},{"t":"ItemFormat","g":"Nexus API Index","u":"graph-nexus/api.html#ItemFormat"},{"t":"Combo","g":"Nexus API Index","u":"graph-nexus/api.html#Combo"},{"t":"Graph","g":"Nexus API Index","u":"graph-nexus/api.html#Graph"},{"t":"TimeBar","g":"Nexus API Index","u":"graph-nexus/api.html#TimeBar"},{"t":"TimeBarOptions","g":"Nexus API Index","u":"graph-nexus/api.html#TimeBarOptions"},{"t":"TimeBarEvents","g":"Nexus API Index","u":"graph-nexus/api.html#TimeBarEvents"},{"t":"Map","g":"Nexus API Index","u":"graph-nexus/api.html#Map"},{"t":"MapOptions","g":"Nexus API Index","u":"graph-nexus/api.html#MapOptions"},{"t":"API Signatures","g":"Overview","u":"graph-nexus/api-signatures.html","x":"API Signatures Every public method and property, with its TypeScript signature from graph-nexus.d.ts (shipped in the package). Option and item shapes are in the typings and on the API Index . Methods that return a Promise also accept a trailing callback, for example chart.layout('organic', {}, () => {}) . GraphNexus # The GraphNexus object: creates components and holds global settings. GraphNexus.create # GraphNexus.createTimeline # GraphNexus.components # GraphNexus.callbacks # GraphNexus.coords # GraphNexus.corsAdapter # GraphNexus.dashedLineSupport # GraphNexus.fullScreenCapable # GraphNexus.getFontIcon # GraphNexus.getGraphEngine # GraphNexus.info # GraphNexus.mode # GraphNexus.paths # images: base for node image URLs; assets: base URL of the assets/ folder (bundled Leaflet) for dist builds. GraphNexus.setSize # GraphNexus.toggleFullScreen # GraphNexus.webGLSupport # Chart # A chart, returned by GraphNexus.create() . Chart.load # Chart.merge # Chart.expand # Chart.setItem # Chart.removeItem # Chart.getItem # Chart.clear # Chart.serialize # Chart.each # Chart.selection # Chart.setProperties # Chart.animateProperties # Chart.hide # Chart.show # Chart.filter # Chart.foreground # C"},{"t":"GraphNexus","g":"API Signatures","u":"graph-nexus/api-signatures.html#GraphNexus"},{"t":"Chart","g":"API Signatures","u":"graph-nexus/api-signatures.html#Chart"},{"t":"Graph","g":"API Signatures","u":"graph-nexus/api-signatures.html#Graph"},{"t":"Combo","g":"API Signatures","u":"graph-nexus/api-signatures.html#Combo"},{"t":"Map","g":"API Signatures","u":"graph-nexus/api-signatures.html#Map"},{"t":"TimeBar","g":"API Signatures","u":"graph-nexus/api-signatures.html#TimeBar"},{"t":"Installing Nexus","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/installing-graph-nexus.html","x":"Installing Nexus Introduction # Nexus ships as a single npm package, graph-nexus , with no runtime dependencies. The package contains ES modules, a CommonJS build, a UMD bundle for script tags, TypeScript typings, React / Vue / Angular wrappers and the bundled assets/ (icons, flags, fonts, Leaflet for map mode). Step 1: Get the package # Nexus is distributed as a tarball, graph-nexus-1.0.0.tgz . Use the tarball supplied by the Nexus team, or build it yourself from the Nexus source repository: Step 2: Add Nexus to your project # Copy the tarball into your project directory, then add it as a dependency: This adds an entry like \"graph-nexus\": \"file:graph-nexus-1.0.0.tgz\" to your package.json . Commit the tarball (in a private repository) or host it on a private registry so that every developer and CI build installs the same version. See Obfuscation Requirements before making it reachable from a public location. Step 3: Import Nexus # Framework wrappers are exposed as sub-paths: graph-nexus/react , graph-nexus/vue and graph-nexus/angular . See Importing Nexus for details. Without a bundler: script tag # Copy node_modules/graph-nexus/dist/graph-nexus.umd.js (and the assets/ folder if yo"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/installing-graph-nexus.html#intro"},{"t":"Step 1: Get the package","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/installing-graph-nexus.html#get-package"},{"t":"Step 2: Add Nexus to your project","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/installing-graph-nexus.html#npm"},{"t":"Step 3: Import Nexus","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/installing-graph-nexus.html#import"},{"t":"Without a bundler: script tag","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/installing-graph-nexus.html#script-tag"},{"t":"Step 4: Check the installation","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/installing-graph-nexus.html#verify"},{"t":"Next steps","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/installing-graph-nexus.html#next"},{"t":"Importing Nexus","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/importing-graph-nexus.html","x":"Importing Nexus Introduction # Once you've installed Nexus as a package, you can include it in your project just like other Javascript libraries. Nexus supports the two main types of module loaders (ES6 modules and CommonJS/UMD modules) by including two separate bundles. Import using ES6 imports # Import Nexus using the 'graph-nexus' module specifier: Import using CommonJS/UMD # Require Nexus using the 'graph-nexus/umd' module specifier or its alias 'graph-nexus' : For backwards compatibility and for use in script tags, this CommonJS/UMD module also sets a GraphNexus global variable where ever its used. For example:"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Importing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/importing-graph-nexus.html#intro"},{"t":"Import using ES6 imports","g":"Importing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/importing-graph-nexus.html#import1"},{"t":"Import using CommonJS/UMD","g":"Importing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/importing-graph-nexus.html#import2"},{"t":"Nexus and TypeScript","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/graph-nexus-and-typescript.html","x":"Nexus and TypeScript Introduction # Nexus supports the use of TypeScript . You can write your application in TypeScript to make use of features such as intellisense, compile-time type checking and context-sensitive API documentation. TypeScript is a strongly typed, open source superset of JavaScript that is compiled to simple JavaScript and runs on any browser. It features type annotations that let you record contracts of functions and variables in a lightweight way. It lets you use existing JavaScript code, import other JavaScript libraries, and call TypeScript code from JavaScript. We regularly test our definition files against the latest available TypeScript version. The minimum version of TypeScript supported by Nexus is TypeScript 4.2. Older versions may function as well but they haven't been tested and are not officially supported. Using TypeScript with Nexus # When using JavaScript libraries (such as Nexus) with TypeScript, it’s useful to have the type definitions for each library. Nexus comes with detailed type definitions that are updated every time there's a change to the Nexus API. Including these definitions in your project makes coding in TypeScript much easier. The ea"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Nexus and TypeScript","u":"graph-nexus/installing/graph-nexus-and-typescript.html#intro"},{"t":"Using TypeScript with Nexus","g":"Nexus and TypeScript","u":"graph-nexus/installing/graph-nexus-and-typescript.html#using-with-graph-nexus"},{"t":"Using Nexus type definitions","g":"Nexus and TypeScript","u":"graph-nexus/installing/graph-nexus-and-typescript.html#definitions"},{"t":"Upgrading","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/upgrading.html","x":"There are many ways of upgrading Nexus - this document covers two of the most common. Contact the Nexus team if you need any help with your upgrade process. Upgrading Nexus versioning # It’s worth understanding the difference between major and minor Nexus versions, because it affects the steps you should take before you upgrade. We use a format similar to semantic versioning , where releases are numbered in the format major.minor : Major versions (e.g., x.0) are released roughly once a year. In exceptional cases, they may contain breaking changes. Minor versions (e.g., x.1, x.2, etc.) are released regularly throughout the year. They contain backwards-compatible features and fixes. Before you upgrade # It's important to read the Release Notes for details of new and updated features, enhancements, and bug fixes. Also check the deprecated items that we plan to stop supporting in the next major release. You can check the details of your current version using GraphNexus.info() to work out what's changed. Before you upgrade to a major version, it’s also important to check for: Breaking changes End of support notices in the Release Notes If you can’t upgrade to the latest major version ri"},{"t":"Nexus versioning","g":"Upgrading","u":"graph-nexus/installing/upgrading.html#intro"},{"t":"Before you upgrade","g":"Upgrading","u":"graph-nexus/installing/upgrading.html#beforeUpgrading"},{"t":"How to upgrade","g":"Upgrading","u":"graph-nexus/installing/upgrading.html#howTo"},{"t":"Troubleshooting","g":"Upgrading","u":"graph-nexus/installing/upgrading.html#troubleshootingErrors"},{"t":"Using Nexus with Angular","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-angular.html","x":"Using Nexus with Angular Introduction # This quick start adds an interactive Nexus chart to a Angular app, using the Angular components shipped in the Nexus package. See the Angular Reference for every property and event. Requirements # A current Node.js LTS. Check with node -v . Step 1: Create an Angular app # Step 2: Add Nexus to the app # Build the Nexus package from the Nexus repository (or use a tarball you were given): Then, from the my-app directory, install it and copy the bundled icons into the app's public folder: The Angular components are published as TypeScript source, so the Angular compiler builds them with your app. Step 3: Create a chart # Replace src/app/app.component.ts (remove the generated app.component.html if your component file points to it) with: Give the chart a size in src/styles.css : Run ng serve --open . You should see the network laid out, and you can drag, pan and zoom. Step 4: Respond to clicks # Clicking a node now puts it and its neighbours in the foreground; clicking the background restores every item. chart.getItem() checks the clicked id, chart.graph().neighbours() finds connected nodes and chart.foreground() fades the rest. The chart object pa"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Using Nexus with Angular","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-angular.html#intro"},{"t":"Requirements","g":"Using Nexus with Angular","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-angular.html#requirements"},{"t":"Step 1: Create an Angular app","g":"Using Nexus with Angular","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-angular.html#create"},{"t":"Step 2: Add Nexus to the app","g":"Using Nexus with Angular","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-angular.html#install"},{"t":"Step 3: Create a chart","g":"Using Nexus with Angular","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-angular.html#chart"},{"t":"Step 4: Respond to clicks","g":"Using Nexus with Angular","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-angular.html#next"},{"t":"Using Nexus with React","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-react.html","x":"Using Nexus with React Introduction # This quick start adds an interactive Nexus chart to a React app, using the React components shipped in the Nexus package. See the React Reference for every property and event. Requirements # A current Node.js LTS. Check with node -v . Step 1: Create a React app # Step 2: Add Nexus to the app # Build the Nexus package from the Nexus repository (or use a tarball you were given): Then, from the my-app directory, install it and copy the bundled icons into the app's public folder: Step 3: Create a chart # Replace src/App.jsx with: Give the chart a size in src/App.css . The component fills its parent, so the parent or the className must have a height: Run npm run dev . You should see the network laid out, and you can drag, pan and zoom. Step 4: Respond to clicks # Clicking a node now puts it and its neighbours in the foreground; clicking the background restores every item. chart.getItem() checks the clicked id, chart.graph().neighbours() finds connected nodes and chart.foreground() fades the rest. The chart object passed to the ready handler is the full Chart API , so anything in the API reference can be called on it."},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Using Nexus with React","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-react.html#intro"},{"t":"Requirements","g":"Using Nexus with React","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-react.html#requirements"},{"t":"Step 1: Create a React app","g":"Using Nexus with React","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-react.html#create"},{"t":"Step 2: Add Nexus to the app","g":"Using Nexus with React","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-react.html#install"},{"t":"Step 3: Create a chart","g":"Using Nexus with React","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-react.html#chart"},{"t":"Step 4: Respond to clicks","g":"Using Nexus with React","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-react.html#next"},{"t":"Using Nexus with Rollup","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-rollup.html","x":"Using Nexus with Rollup Introduction # This quick start guide shows how to create a simple Nexus visualisation with Rollup.js . When you are finished, you will have a simple interactive Nexus chart. Note that if you store Nexus in your source control you must ensure it's not publicly available. Read more about the Obfuscation Requirements before deploying your application. Requirements # To follow this tutorial, you should have the latest version of Node.js installed. To check your version, you can run node -v in the console. Step 1: Create a new package # Create a new directory named my-app and create a new npm package from this directory: In the terminal from the my-app directory, add Rollup as a dependency: Step 2: Create a boilerplate application # Create a new src folder inside the my-app directory. Inside my-app/src , create a new main.js file with the following contents: The main.js file will eventually contain our application code. Back inside the main my-app directory, create a new rollup.config.js file containing Rollup configuration. This will take our application code and bundle it: Also inside the my-app directory, modify the existing package.json file to include a scr"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Using Nexus with Rollup","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-rollup.html#intro"},{"t":"Requirements","g":"Using Nexus with Rollup","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-rollup.html#requirements"},{"t":"Step 1: Create a new package","g":"Using Nexus with Rollup","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-rollup.html#step1"},{"t":"Step 2: Create a boilerplate application","g":"Using Nexus with Rollup","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-rollup.html#step2"},{"t":"Step 3: Run a web server","g":"Using Nexus with Rollup","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-rollup.html#step3"},{"t":"Step 4: Create a chart","g":"Using Nexus with Rollup","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-rollup.html#step4"},{"t":"Step 5: Minify your bundle and create source maps (optional)","g":"Using Nexus with Rollup","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-rollup.html#step5"},{"t":"Using Nexus with Vue","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-vue.html","x":"Using Nexus with Vue Introduction # This quick start adds an interactive Nexus chart to a Vue app, using the Vue components shipped in the Nexus package. See the Vue Reference for every property and event. Requirements # A current Node.js LTS. Check with node -v . Step 1: Create a Vue app # You don't need any of the optional features for this tutorial. Step 2: Add Nexus to the app # Build the Nexus package from the Nexus repository (or use a tarball you were given): Then, from the my-app directory, install it and copy the bundled icons into the app's public folder: Step 3: Create a chart # Replace src/App.vue with: Run npm run dev . You should see the network laid out, and you can drag, pan and zoom. Step 4: Respond to clicks # Clicking a node now puts it and its neighbours in the foreground; clicking the background restores every item. chart.getItem() checks the clicked id, chart.graph().neighbours() finds connected nodes and chart.foreground() fades the rest. The chart object passed to the ready handler is the full Chart API , so anything in the API reference can be called on it."},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Using Nexus with Vue","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-vue.html#intro"},{"t":"Requirements","g":"Using Nexus with Vue","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-vue.html#requirements"},{"t":"Step 1: Create a Vue app","g":"Using Nexus with Vue","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-vue.html#create"},{"t":"Step 2: Add Nexus to the app","g":"Using Nexus with Vue","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-vue.html#install"},{"t":"Step 3: Create a chart","g":"Using Nexus with Vue","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-vue.html#chart"},{"t":"Step 4: Respond to clicks","g":"Using Nexus with Vue","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-vue.html#next"},{"t":"Using Nexus with Webpack","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-webpack.html","x":"Using Nexus with Webpack Introduction # This quick start guide shows how to create a simple Nexus visualisation with Webpack . When you are finished, you will have a simple interactive Nexus chart. Note that if you store Nexus in your source control you must ensure it's not publicly available. Read more about the Obfuscation Requirements before deploying your application. Requirements # To follow this tutorial, you should have the latest version of Node.js installed. To check your version, you can run node -v in the console. Step 1: Create a new package # Create a new directory named my-app and create a new npm package from this directory: In the terminal from the my-app directory, add the following Webpack dependencies: Dependency Description Webpack The Webpack module bundler. webpack-cli Adds a set of commands for Webpack configuration. html-webpack-plugin Simplifies creating HTML output files to be served to the generated bundles. webpack-dev-server Allows using Webpack with a live-reloading development server. webpack-merge Concatenates arrays and merges configuration objects to help manage configuration. Step 2: Create a boilerplate application # Create a new src folder insid"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Using Nexus with Webpack","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-webpack.html#intro"},{"t":"Requirements","g":"Using Nexus with Webpack","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-webpack.html#requirements"},{"t":"Step 1: Create a new package","g":"Using Nexus with Webpack","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-webpack.html#step1"},{"t":"Step 2: Create a boilerplate application","g":"Using Nexus with Webpack","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-webpack.html#step2"},{"t":"Step 3: Run a web server","g":"Using Nexus with Webpack","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-webpack.html#step3"},{"t":"Step 4: Create a chart","g":"Using Nexus with Webpack","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-webpack.html#step4"},{"t":"Step 5: Bundle your files","g":"Using Nexus with Webpack","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-webpack.html#step5"},{"t":"Using Nexus with plain JavaScript","g":"Installing Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-plain-javascript.html","x":"Using Nexus with plain JavaScript Introduction # This quick start guide shows how to integrate a simple Nexus visualisation into an app. When you are finished, you will have a simple interactive Nexus chart. Note that if you store Nexus in your source control you must ensure it's not publicly available. Read more about the Obfuscation Requirements before deploying your application. Step 1: Create a new web page # Create a new directory named my-app and create a new npm package from this directory: Download the Nexus package from the link below and place it in the my-app directory. Download Nexus 7.5.0-6286156683 In the terminal from the my-app directory, add Nexus as a package dependency: Nexus also includes extra dependencies for map mode and PDF export . See their respective documentation for more information on how to use them. Next, add a file my-app/index.html with the following contents: This HTML defines a div where the Nexus chart will later appear. Step 2: Run a web server # You can simply open index.html in a browser directly from the file system, but Nexus needs to run via a web server to display images correctly. You can use any web server you like, but live-server is a"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Using Nexus with plain JavaScript","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-plain-javascript.html#intro"},{"t":"Step 1: Create a new web page","g":"Using Nexus with plain JavaScript","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-plain-javascript.html#step1"},{"t":"Step 2: Run a web server","g":"Using Nexus with plain JavaScript","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-plain-javascript.html#step2"},{"t":"Step 3: Create a chart","g":"Using Nexus with plain JavaScript","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-plain-javascript.html#step3"},{"t":"Step 4: Create a network","g":"Using Nexus with plain JavaScript","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-plain-javascript.html#step4"},{"t":"Step 5: Add event handling","g":"Using Nexus with plain JavaScript","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-plain-javascript.html#step5"},{"t":"Step 6: Update the chart","g":"Using Nexus with plain JavaScript","u":"graph-nexus/installing/using-graph-nexus-with-plain-javascript.html#step6"},{"t":"Architecture","g":"Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/architecture.html","x":"Architecture Nexus architecture is simple, secure and scalable. It doesn't require dedicated visualisation servers or additional security measures. The core of the Nexus architecture is made of a Nexus component and a JavaScript controller . Once you deploy the component to a web server and embed it on a web page, it sends function calls to the controller and receives events back from it. You can customise the controller to trigger the events your users expect when they're working with charts. Here's how Nexus architecture fits together: Compatibility # Nexus is entirely self-contained and can be hosted on any web server. Assets such as icons and images can also be hosted on your server. Security # Nexus doesn't extend any base JavaScript prototypes, define global variables, or depend on any third party libraries. The only features requiring third-party plugins are optional to use. See our Third Party Libraries documentation for details. There is no link maintained between Nexus and any remote server. You can safely include Nexus behind security defences without having to configure and manage access. Read more in our Security documentation. Rendering graphics # Nexus supports: WebG"},{"t":"Compatibility","g":"Architecture","u":"graph-nexus/basics/architecture.html#compatibility"},{"t":"Security","g":"Architecture","u":"graph-nexus/basics/architecture.html#security"},{"t":"Rendering graphics","g":"Architecture","u":"graph-nexus/basics/architecture.html#rendering"},{"t":"Chart Basics","g":"Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/chart-basics.html","x":"Chart Basics Introduction # The Nexus chart is the container for items that are used to build a graph and visualise your data. The main items in graph visualisations are nodes and links . Both nodes and links can have sub-items with additional information. For example, nodes can have labels , glyphs , halos , donuts and more. Links can have labels , glyphs , arrows and more. The chart itself can also contain different styling or navigation elements such as logos, watermarks, navigation controls or overview window. Creating charts # To create a chart, call the GraphNexus.create() function: Make sure that you wait for GraphNexus.create() to finish before calling other chart functions such as chart.load() . See Asynchronous JavaScript for more details. For detailed guides on creating an app with Nexus, take a look at our JavaScript , React , Angular , Vue , Webpack or Rollup tutorials. Styling charts # For nodes you can use circles, rectangles, font icons or images. You can combine them with glyphs, text labels and font icons to achieve virtually any design. Links can have arrows and can be dashed, dotted, curved, coloured with a gradient or animated with a flow. You can also combine "},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Chart Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/chart-basics.html#introcharts"},{"t":"Creating charts","g":"Chart Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/chart-basics.html#creatingcharts"},{"t":"Styling charts","g":"Chart Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/chart-basics.html#stylingcharts"},{"t":"Loading data into charts","g":"Chart Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/chart-basics.html#loadingdataintocharts"},{"t":"Combos","g":"Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/combos.html","x":"Combos Introduction # Data-heavy visualisations are often complex. To reduce clutter, you can combine multiple nodes into a single combined node. We call this a combo. Links are combined too. Notice how Debbie's links to Diane and Bob combine automatically into a single link, referred to as a combo link. Combos have two states: open or closed. When they're open, you can see and interact with the items inside the combo border. Creating and removing combos # There are two ways to create combos, each of which can be useful for different tasks: Combine the existing nodes using chart.combo().combine() function. Assign parentId to nodes as they are added to the chart with load , merge and expand . Combining existing nodes using chart.combo().combine() # Use this approach for creating combos after the data is loaded, for example when you want to allow users to create their own combos. The created combos are automatically assigned an id when they are created, prefixed with '_combo_' for nodes and '_combolink_' for links. Assigning a parentId to nodes as they are loaded # The parentId property of nodes can be set when loading data into the chart to define a combo hierarchy. This method can "},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Combos","u":"graph-nexus/basics/combos.html#intro"},{"t":"Creating and removing combos","g":"Combos","u":"graph-nexus/basics/combos.html#create"},{"t":"Open and closed combos","g":"Combos","u":"graph-nexus/basics/combos.html#open"},{"t":"Layouts inside open combos","g":"Combos","u":"graph-nexus/basics/combos.html#layouts"},{"t":"Adapting to changes","g":"Combos","u":"graph-nexus/basics/combos.html#comboAdapt"},{"t":"Transferring nodes between combos","g":"Combos","u":"graph-nexus/basics/combos.html#transfer"},{"t":"Styling for combos","g":"Combos","u":"graph-nexus/basics/combos.html#style"},{"t":"Resizing combos","g":"Combos","u":"graph-nexus/basics/combos.html#resize"},{"t":"Revealing links","g":"Combos","u":"graph-nexus/basics/combos.html#reveal"},{"t":"Nested combos","g":"Combos","u":"graph-nexus/basics/combos.html#nested"},{"t":"Finding items","g":"Combos","u":"graph-nexus/basics/combos.html#find"},{"t":"Combos and the Nexus API","g":"Combos","u":"graph-nexus/basics/combos.html#combosAndApi"},{"t":"See also","g":"Combos","u":"graph-nexus/basics/combos.html#seeAlso"},{"t":"Events Basics","g":"Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/events-basics.html","x":"Events Basics Introduction # Nexus lets you respond to virtually any event on the chart or time bar elements. To attach a handler function to an event, you can use the chart.on() and timebar.on() methods. To detach them, use chart.off() and timebar.off() . For a list of events, see the Chart Events API Reference and Time Bar Events API Reference . If you want to use events in map mode, read more in our Map mode events documentation. Handling events # To add an event handler, create an event handler function for the event and attach it using the on() function. When an event fires, any attached handler functions are passed a single object containing all the event details. You can use destructuring to find the properties you want: To detach the event handler, use the off() function and specify which handler and/or event should be detached. If no handler is supplied, all handlers for the specified event are detached. If no event name is supplied, all event handlers for all events are detached. Overriding default actions # Some Nexus events come with default behaviours. For example, dragging a node will move it, clicking on a link will select it, or double-clicking the chart background "},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Events Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/events-basics.html#introevents"},{"t":"Handling events","g":"Events Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/events-basics.html#handlingevents"},{"t":"Overriding default actions","g":"Events Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/events-basics.html#overridingactions"},{"t":"Legacy bind() events","g":"Events Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/events-basics.html#legacyevents"},{"t":"Layout Basics","g":"Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/layout-basics.html","x":"Layout Basics Introduction # Layouts in Nexus let you display your data in a clear and effective format. There are several different layouts, each with multiple customisation options. As new nodes are initially given default coordinates, it's almost always a good idea to call chart.layout() after you have loaded in data to avoid nodes being on top of one another. If you're adding new data and want to keep the approximate position of existing nodes, consider using chart.expand() . See also the layout options API which you can use to customise your layouts. Organic # The organic layout is our default layout that is used when you don't specify a layout while running chart.layout() or chart.expand() . Organic is a force-directed layout, which means that the layout places connected nodes closer together and organises them in a way that reduces link crossings in the chart as much as possible. It is clear, reliable and makes a good all-rounder for any type or size of data as it focuses on finding patterns and symmetries. In addition, organic is especially useful for untangling complex networks as it spreads nodes and links apart. Organic can be run as a short layout in adaptive mode which"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Layout Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/layout-basics.html#intro"},{"t":"Organic","g":"Layout Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/layout-basics.html#organicstandard"},{"t":"Sequential","g":"Layout Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/layout-basics.html#trees"},{"t":"Structural","g":"Layout Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/layout-basics.html#structural"},{"t":"Radial","g":"Layout Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/layout-basics.html#radial"},{"t":"Lens","g":"Layout Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/layout-basics.html#lens"},{"t":"Layout Options","g":"Layout Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/layout-basics.html#options"},{"t":"Arrangements - Layouts inside Combos","g":"Layout Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/layout-basics.html#arrange"},{"t":"Loading Data","g":"Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/loading-data.html","x":"Loading Data Nexus only accepts data in JSON format: Due to the variety of data types, there is no tool for automatic data conversion in Nexus. You can convert and parse your data either server-side or client-side. Nexus is generally tolerant of bad data where possible. Invalid values for properties are usually treated as undefined and the remaining valid data is used to build the graph. Loading data into a chart # Once your data is in the Nexus JSON format, call either chart.load() , chart.merge() or chart.expand() and pass the data object as the first argument: Make sure that you've created a chart with GraphNexus.create() before calling any of these functions. chart.load() loads the specified data and deletes any other data from the chart. This is useful when loading data initially when a user first imports data. chart.merge() adds new items to the chart or modifies the properties of existing items. If the merged data contains any items with already existing ids, the properties of existing items will be replaced with the new properties. chart.expand() runs a merge followed by a layout. You can specify the layout in the chart.expand() options . It's usually a good idea to run a l"},{"t":"Loading data into a chart","g":"Loading Data","u":"graph-nexus/basics/loading-data.html#loading-data-into-a-chart"},{"t":"Database compatibility","g":"Loading Data","u":"graph-nexus/basics/loading-data.html#database-compatibility"},{"t":"Map Mode","g":"Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/map-mode.html","x":"Map Mode Introduction # Map mode in Nexus allows you to analyse geospatial data. As long as graph data has latitude and longitude location properties, users can visualise chart items on a map. Map mode is also compatible with any map tile provider. Map Tile Examples # Nexus and Leaflet # To support map mode, you need to load Leaflet.js into the page before showing the map. We recommend using the version bundled in the ./map directory of the nexusgraph package. You can include the graph-nexus/assets/vendor/leaflet/leaflet.js JS bundle and graph-nexus/assets/vendor/leaflet/leaflet.css stylesheet in your app as HTML tags: Alternatively, you can include the JS bundle and stylesheet using a bundler such as Webpack . As Leaflet controls the chart background in map mode, many of the options controlling the view do not alter the chart in map mode. See Special behaviour for more information. If Nexus detects a Leaflet version older than 1.3.4, it'll throw an exception and the map won't be drawn. If a version between 1.3.4 - 1.9.0 is found, Nexus draws the map and generates a warning in the developer console. If a version newer than 1.9.1 is found, Nexus draws the map and generates a warning"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Map Mode","u":"graph-nexus/basics/map-mode.html#intro"},{"t":"Nexus and Leaflet","g":"Map Mode","u":"graph-nexus/basics/map-mode.html#leaflet"},{"t":"Switching to map mode","g":"Map Mode","u":"graph-nexus/basics/map-mode.html#basics"},{"t":"Map Layers","g":"Map Mode","u":"graph-nexus/basics/map-mode.html#maplayers"},{"t":"Converting latitude and longitude to view coordinates","g":"Map Mode","u":"graph-nexus/basics/map-mode.html#coords"},{"t":"Special behaviour","g":"Map Mode","u":"graph-nexus/basics/map-mode.html#specialBehaviour"},{"t":"See also","g":"Map Mode","u":"graph-nexus/basics/map-mode.html#seeAlso"},{"t":"Time Bar","g":"Basics","u":"graph-nexus/basics/time-bar.html","x":"Time Bar Introduction # The Nexus time bar lets users quickly spot how connected data evolves over time. It offers powerful ways to filter data through events on both the chart and time bar: Chart-driven filtering : select the chart items of interest so the time bar shows when they were involved in events. Time bar-driven filtering : focus on the time an event occurred so the chart shows which items were involved. Key features # There are two ways that you can display data in the time bar: Histogram # Each bar represents a period in time and is a cumulative count of all items within the range. Selection lines do the same, but on a different (usually sub)set of data. Histogram bars can also display groups of data within them as a set of stacked bars. Area Plot # Events and durations are represented as a distribution, with the value trailing off to each side. The height of the area at each point is a total of these distribution values, which creates a smooth shaded area. As events are discrete, the height of the line only represents an estimate of the value at each point in time. The area under the curve will be filled. Selection lines # Selection lines also count items within a rang"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Time Bar","u":"graph-nexus/basics/time-bar.html#intro"},{"t":"Key features","g":"Time Bar","u":"graph-nexus/basics/time-bar.html#features"},{"t":"Getting time-based data into Nexus","g":"Time Bar","u":"graph-nexus/basics/time-bar.html#importing"},{"t":"Connecting chart and time bar data","g":"Time Bar","u":"graph-nexus/basics/time-bar.html#connecting"},{"t":"See also","g":"Time Bar","u":"graph-nexus/basics/time-bar.html#seealso"},{"t":"3D View","g":"Features","u":"graph-nexus/features/3d-view.html","x":"3D View The 3D view draws the chart through an orbit camera. Nodes take an optional z coordinate, and users can rotate the network to pull apart clusters that overlap in 2D. It is the same chart: items, styles, events and the rest of the API work as before. The Enron network from the Centrality demo in 3D. Nearer nodes draw larger and on top. Turning on the 3D View # Set the chart view3d option and run a force layout: Turning it off restores the 2D picture; z values are kept but ignored. With a level camera and every z at 0, the 3D view draws the same picture as 2D, apart from arrowheads (see Links and Arrowheads ). The same network in 2D, for comparison. Node Depth # Give nodes a z property in world units. Positive z is away from the viewer when the camera is level. Nodes without z sit at 0. The Camera # chart.camera() returns the camera, and chart.camera(values, { animate, time }) changes it: Property Meaning yaw Turn about the vertical axis, in radians. pitch Tilt up or down, in radians, kept just short of ±90°. focal Distance from the eye to the orbit pivot, in world units (default 1500). Smaller values give stronger perspective. targetX , targetY , targetZ The orbit pivot. zoo"},{"t":"Turning on the 3D View","g":"3D View","u":"graph-nexus/features/3d-view.html#enable"},{"t":"Node Depth","g":"3D View","u":"graph-nexus/features/3d-view.html#nodes"},{"t":"The Camera","g":"3D View","u":"graph-nexus/features/3d-view.html#camera"},{"t":"Interaction","g":"3D View","u":"graph-nexus/features/3d-view.html#interaction"},{"t":"Layouts","g":"3D View","u":"graph-nexus/features/3d-view.html#layouts"},{"t":"Links and Arrowheads","g":"3D View","u":"graph-nexus/features/3d-view.html#links"},{"t":"Appearance Options","g":"3D View","u":"graph-nexus/features/3d-view.html#appearance"},{"t":"Canvas and WebGL","g":"3D View","u":"graph-nexus/features/3d-view.html#rendering"},{"t":"Limitations","g":"3D View","u":"graph-nexus/features/3d-view.html#limits"},{"t":"Aggregate Links","g":"Features","u":"graph-nexus/features/aggregate-links.html","x":"Aggregate Links Introduction # When charts contain large numbers of links between individual pairs of nodes or combos, it can become difficult to really understand the connections and to analyse the chart. To solve this, you can set up your chart to display groups of links between pairs of nodes as aggregate links. Each aggregate link represents a group of one or multiple links, which reduces the number of connections drawn in the chart. You can also style aggregate links to show information such as the number, or type, of underlying child links. Aggregate link functionality is currently in beta . Aggregate links are a visual representation of the child links they represent. The child links inside an aggregate link aren't removed or marked as hidden , but are simply not displayed as individual links. Enabling aggregate links # To enable link aggregation within your chart, set the aggregateLinks chart option. For example, to simply aggregate all links between pairs of nodes: You might want to aggregate just the links that have a particular property. Suppose your links all have a type property defined within their d property, then to aggregate links with that property: Your links mig"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Aggregate Links","u":"graph-nexus/features/aggregate-links.html#intro"},{"t":"Enabling aggregate links","g":"Aggregate Links","u":"graph-nexus/features/aggregate-links.html#enable"},{"t":"Styling","g":"Aggregate Links","u":"graph-nexus/features/aggregate-links.html#style"},{"t":"Obtaining aggregation information","g":"Aggregate Links","u":"graph-nexus/features/aggregate-links.html#identify"},{"t":"Inheriting state from child links","g":"Aggregate Links","u":"graph-nexus/features/aggregate-links.html#inheritance"},{"t":"Interactions with other features","g":"Aggregate Links","u":"graph-nexus/features/aggregate-links.html#interactions"},{"t":"Chart Export","g":"Features","u":"graph-nexus/features/chart-export.html","x":"Chart Export Introduction # Exporting charts as images can be useful for presenting, sharing, printing or keeping digital records of your charts. To ensure the most versatile use, exporting should meet certain criteria: Useful format - Offers popular and easily accessible format types such as SVG or PDF. High quality output - Requirements may vary depending on the use case, but digital use may require high resolution or vector quality. Customisability - The content being exported should be customisable e.g. to fit the size needs or to integrate into reports or presentations. The chart.export() function, currently in beta, offers all of the above in both raster (PNG, JPEG) and vector (SVG, PDF) format. The chart.export function also supports exporting to PDF document format. Exporting to image formats # The chart.export() function offers export to PNG, JPEG and SVG image file formats. For more details about exporting to PDF document format, see a separate PDF Export page. The extents option lets you specify the exported content: 'view' - exports the current on-screen view, where the default aspect ratio is determined by the on-screen view 'chart' - exports the whole chart, where the"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Chart Export","u":"graph-nexus/features/chart-export.html#intro"},{"t":"Exporting to image formats","g":"Chart Export","u":"graph-nexus/features/chart-export.html#export-image"},{"t":"Combos Concepts","g":"Features","u":"graph-nexus/features/combos-concepts.html","x":"Combos Concepts Introduction # This page discusses the key concepts behind the combos feature in Nexus 4.0 or later. The design for combos is different to other Nexus features, so understanding what’s happening under the hood will help you get to grips with the feature more quickly. This page isn’t an introduction to what you can do with combos. The Combos page covers that, so we recommend you read that first. Two different approaches to data # One of the most important concepts to understand when working with combos is the difference between a ‘top level’ and an ‘underlying level’ approach to the data. The approach you take depends on what kind of data you’re visualising. For example, look at this network. Nodes b and c are children of a parent combo: To identify the shortest path between nodes a and d, you have two options: ignore the combo parent and take the links between the child nodes, b and c, into account. This is the underlying level approach. treat the combo parent as a single entity and ignore the links between the child nodes b and c. This is the top level approach. Let’s consider these options in more detail. Underlying level approach # The underlying level approach i"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Combos Concepts","u":"graph-nexus/features/combos-concepts.html#intro"},{"t":"Two different approaches to data","g":"Combos Concepts","u":"graph-nexus/features/combos-concepts.html#approaches"},{"t":"Iterating over items","g":"Combos Concepts","u":"graph-nexus/features/combos-concepts.html#iterating"},{"t":"Controlling which items are visible","g":"Combos Concepts","u":"graph-nexus/features/combos-concepts.html#visible"},{"t":"Running graph analysis","g":"Combos Concepts","u":"graph-nexus/features/combos-concepts.html#graph"},{"t":"Fonts and Font Icons","g":"Features","u":"graph-nexus/features/fonts-and-font-icons.html","x":"Fonts and Font Icons Introduction # Nexus lets you use custom fonts to match the fonts used in your applications. In addition, you can also add font icons to various items in the chart. Using fonts # Nexus uses 'sans-serif' as the default font family. To set a custom default font family, pass the name in the fontFamily chart option. To set a font family for a chart item such as a node, link, label or a glyph, you can also set the ff property of the item. Using font icons # While images offer you more reliable cross-browser support, font icons offers better scalability and let you change the font icon colour dynamically. Adding font icons # To add a font icon, pass the Unicode value to the t property of the fi property of the node or glyph. You can use GraphNexus.getFontIcon() to look up a Unicode value from a class name. For example, using Font Awesome, a font icon on a node would look like this: Unless a font family is explicitly set on the item via the ff property, the icon will be rendered from the family specified in the IconFontFamily option. Custom font families # Follow these steps to use custom font families in Nexus: Download and reference the TypeKit WebFontLoader or link"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Fonts and Font Icons","u":"graph-nexus/features/fonts-and-font-icons.html#intro"},{"t":"Using fonts","g":"Fonts and Font Icons","u":"graph-nexus/features/fonts-and-font-icons.html#usingfonts"},{"t":"Using font icons","g":"Fonts and Font Icons","u":"graph-nexus/features/fonts-and-font-icons.html#usingfonticons"},{"t":"Troubleshooting","g":"Fonts and Font Icons","u":"graph-nexus/features/fonts-and-font-icons.html#fontproblems"},{"t":"Graph Centrality","g":"Features","u":"graph-nexus/features/graph-centrality.html","x":"Graph Centrality Introduction # Nexus has many different analysis algorithms that you can use to identify important aspects of your network. Social network analysis (SNA) or centrality measures are a vital tool for understanding the behaviour of networks and graphs. These algorithms use graph theory to calculate the importance of any given node. Each centrality measure has strengths and weaknesses in identifying features of a network, so it is important to choose the right one. Degrees # Degree is the simplest form of centrality, and assigns scores to nodes based purely on the number of links held by each node. It tells us how many direct, ‘one hop’ connections each node has to other nodes within the network. It is used for finding very connected individuals, popular individuals, individuals who are likely to hold most information or individuals who can quickly connect with the wider network. Directed graph where links of any direction are included in the calculations ( direction:'any' ). Betweenness # Betweenness centrality measures the number of times a node lies on the shortest path between other nodes. This measure shows which nodes act as ‘bridges’ between nodes in a connected"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Graph Centrality","u":"graph-nexus/features/graph-centrality.html#intro"},{"t":"Degrees","g":"Graph Centrality","u":"graph-nexus/features/graph-centrality.html#degrees"},{"t":"Betweenness","g":"Graph Centrality","u":"graph-nexus/features/graph-centrality.html#betweenness"},{"t":"Closeness","g":"Graph Centrality","u":"graph-nexus/features/graph-centrality.html#closeness"},{"t":"EigenCentrality","g":"Graph Centrality","u":"graph-nexus/features/graph-centrality.html#eigencentrality"},{"t":"PageRank","g":"Graph Centrality","u":"graph-nexus/features/graph-centrality.html#pagerank"},{"t":"Graph Engine","g":"Features","u":"graph-nexus/features/graph-engine.html","x":"Graph Engine Introduction # The graph engine powers the analysis features in Nexus. It keeps these features separate from the methods used to render charts. This means you can run graph traversals and Social Network Analysis computation without having to display every chart item first. Graph Engine Advantages # There are many advantages to using the graph engine, particularly when working with large datasets: You can carry out analysis on a subset of the rendered graph. It's quicker and easier to carry out graph traversals such as shortestPath, and social network analysis metrics, including degrees or betweenness. It's integrated into Nexus already, so there's no need to add extra files. You don't have to manipulate the chart to run graph analysis. Getting started # In these code samples, the Graph Engine performs computation tasks without rendering the chart. Step 1 - Create a Graph Engine instance: Step 2 - Use the Nexus Graph Engine API to load the data: You can also load a saved chart (or serialize the current one) in the Graph Engine instance: Step 3 - Perform the graph traversals or SNA computations: Graph Engines in NodeJS # You can use the graph engine in any javascript env"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Graph Engine","u":"graph-nexus/features/graph-engine.html#intro"},{"t":"Graph Engine Advantages","g":"Graph Engine","u":"graph-nexus/features/graph-engine.html#advantages"},{"t":"Getting started","g":"Graph Engine","u":"graph-nexus/features/graph-engine.html#gettingstarted"},{"t":"Graph Engines in NodeJS","g":"Graph Engine","u":"graph-nexus/features/graph-engine.html#nodejs"},{"t":"See Also","g":"Graph Engine","u":"graph-nexus/features/graph-engine.html#seealso"},{"t":"Node and Combo Styling","g":"Features","u":"graph-nexus/features/node-and-combo-styling.html","x":"Node and Combo Styling Introduction # In charts, nodes can represent various concrete or abstract entities such as people, locations, servers or accounts, and can carry various levels of detail and information. Nexus supports a wide range of styling options that let you customise the way nodes look and display information. For quick but impactful changes, you can for example change a node's colour, fill it with an image or a font icon, or add a contrasting border, glyph or a halo. You can find the full list of available APIs in the Node API Reference . More advanced styling in Nexus isn't advanced in the difficulty of implementation, but rather in the complex issues it can help you solve, such as: Large amounts of information or many types of information on a node Dynamically changing data (such as names or numbers in varying length) A need for interactive elements on nodes (such as buttons or editable fields) A specific design language of your application Using the available node styling APIs , you can create custom and high-precision styling for your nodes to tackle any issues. Node shapes and sizes # By default, nodes in Nexus are drawn as circles with a default diameter of 56. "},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Node and Combo Styling","u":"graph-nexus/features/node-and-combo-styling.html#intro"},{"t":"Node shapes and sizes","g":"Node and Combo Styling","u":"graph-nexus/features/node-and-combo-styling.html#rectangularnodes"},{"t":"Advanced label styling","g":"Node and Combo Styling","u":"graph-nexus/features/node-and-combo-styling.html#advancedlabelstyling"},{"t":"Label size and content","g":"Node and Combo Styling","u":"graph-nexus/features/node-and-combo-styling.html#labeltext"},{"t":"Label positioning","g":"Node and Combo Styling","u":"graph-nexus/features/node-and-combo-styling.html#labelpositioning"},{"t":"Multiple inline labels","g":"Node and Combo Styling","u":"graph-nexus/features/node-and-combo-styling.html#multipleinlinelabels"},{"t":"Margin, padding and border","g":"Node and Combo Styling","u":"graph-nexus/features/node-and-combo-styling.html#marginandpadding"},{"t":"Advanced combo styling","g":"Node and Combo Styling","u":"graph-nexus/features/node-and-combo-styling.html#advancedcombostyling"},{"t":"PDF Export","g":"Features","u":"graph-nexus/features/pdf-export.html","x":"PDF Export Introduction # Nexus exports charts to PDF in vector quality with chart.export() . Nodes, links, shapes and labels stay as vector paths and text; images are embedded as JPEG. The same function also exports PNG, JPEG and SVG. See Chart Export . Dependencies # None. The PDF writer is built into Nexus, so there is nothing extra to install, import or load with a script tag. Running PDF export # The result object contains: url - an object URL for the PDF (revoke it when done) blob - the PDF as a Blob of type application/pdf warnings - an array of { type, message } for anything that could not be exported exactly Page size # The PDF has a single page. Its size, in PDF points, is set by fitTo : Not set - the size of the chart on screen. { width, height } - exactly that size. { width } or { height } - that dimension, the other one keeps the on-screen aspect ratio. 'page' - A4 portrait (595 x 842). The chart is drawn to fill the page; there are no margins, headings or multi-page documents. To build a report, put the exported image into a document with your own PDF tooling. Images in PDF # Node images, including SVG images and icons, are embedded as JPEG. Images from another origin"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"PDF Export","u":"graph-nexus/features/pdf-export.html#intro"},{"t":"Dependencies","g":"PDF Export","u":"graph-nexus/features/pdf-export.html#dependencies"},{"t":"Running PDF export","g":"PDF Export","u":"graph-nexus/features/pdf-export.html#running"},{"t":"Images in PDF","g":"PDF Export","u":"graph-nexus/features/pdf-export.html#images"},{"t":"Text in PDF","g":"PDF Export","u":"graph-nexus/features/pdf-export.html#text"},{"t":"Other limits","g":"PDF Export","u":"graph-nexus/features/pdf-export.html#limits"},{"t":"Right-to-Left Text","g":"Features","u":"graph-nexus/features/right-to-left-text.html","x":"Right-to-Left Text Introduction # Nexus supports multiple languages, including right-to-left (RTL) and bidirectional (BIDI) writing systems such as Arabic and Hebrew. When Nexus detects RTL characters, it automatically applies RTL auto support in both WebGL and Canvas to correctly display any RTL and BIDI content. RTL auto support mode # RTL auto support, which is applied by default in Nexus, is a reliable approach for a large scope of cases including: Mixed-system texts with both LTR and RTL characters Complex examples containing punctuation, numbers, emojis or other special characters Strings with symbols in strict functional order such as e-mail addresses or URLs This is because RTL will analyse the individual characters and assign the order dynamically so that only the identified RTL characters are in a RTL direction. This produces the most correct output even for texts with complex combinations of characters. Forced RTL mode in Nexus 6.3 and earlier # Before Nexus 6.4, forced RTL was the recommended approach in both WebGL and Canvas. This was done in one of two ways: By setting the HTML dir attribute to rtl on the Nexus chart element By using CSS to set the direction property "},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Right-to-Left Text","u":"graph-nexus/features/right-to-left-text.html#intro"},{"t":"RTL auto support mode","g":"Right-to-Left Text","u":"graph-nexus/features/right-to-left-text.html#rtlauto"},{"t":"Forced RTL mode in Nexus 6.3 and earlier","g":"Right-to-Left Text","u":"graph-nexus/features/right-to-left-text.html#forcedrtl"},{"t":"Self Links","g":"Features","u":"graph-nexus/features/self-links.html","x":"Self Links A standard link is a connection between two different nodes. A \"self link\" is a single or multiple link from a node back to itself. They can help you to visualise certain scenarios, such as an organisation suspected of fraud that’s sending payments to itself, or direct loopbacks in an IT network. Enabling Self Links # By default, support for self links is disabled and Nexus discards them if they’re loaded onto a chart. To enable support, use the chart selfLinks option. For example: To create a self link, enter the same node id for the link’s id1 and id2 properties: The a1 end of the self link is on the left side of the node; the a2 end is on the upper side. If you want to use self links with the graph engine, see Using Self Links with Graph Functions . Self Link Offsets # The off property of a link specifies how far to offset its midpoint. For a standard link between two nodes, this offset is measured from a straight line. Here's an example of a standard link with its off property set to 50: For a self link, the off property specifies how far the link is offset from its default position. Here's a self link with an off property of 50: If a self link has a zero or unspecif"},{"t":"Enabling Self Links","g":"Self Links","u":"graph-nexus/features/self-links.html#enable"},{"t":"Self Link Offsets","g":"Self Links","u":"graph-nexus/features/self-links.html#offsets"},{"t":"Using Self Links with Graph Functions","g":"Self Links","u":"graph-nexus/features/self-links.html#graph"},{"t":"Time Zones","g":"Features","u":"graph-nexus/features/time-zones.html","x":"Time Zones This page explains how Nexus displays dates and times and handles time zones. About time zones # Every geographical region has its own standard time zone. Each zone has a UTC offset, which is the difference in hours and minutes from Universal Coordinated Time. Many regions observe Daylight Saving Time (DST), where the time offset changes to accommodate differences during the seasons. Offset rules may also change for unpredictable political reasons. Date objects in JavaScript # You can create a Date object using JavaScript's built-in constructor: The browser knows the operating system's current time zone. It uses this to offer conversions to UTC. For example: Most browsers don't allow for changes to time zone rules, so the UTC conversion may not be correct. How Nexus handles time zones # Nexus displays the literal date and time defined in your code: it doesn't try to convert or standardise them. If you want to control how Nexus works with time zones, choose one of the options below. Option One: UTC dates on server and browser # If your users work with servers that record dates in UTC, they'll probably want to see their data in UTC too. Construct your JavaScript dates usin"},{"t":"About time zones","g":"Time Zones","u":"graph-nexus/features/time-zones.html#intro"},{"t":"Date objects in JavaScript","g":"Time Zones","u":"graph-nexus/features/time-zones.html#jstime"},{"t":"How Nexus handles time zones","g":"Time Zones","u":"graph-nexus/features/time-zones.html#kltime"},{"t":"Option One: UTC dates on server and browser","g":"Time Zones","u":"graph-nexus/features/time-zones.html#option1"},{"t":"Option Two: UTC date on server, local time for the user","g":"Time Zones","u":"graph-nexus/features/time-zones.html#option2"},{"t":"Other options","g":"Time Zones","u":"graph-nexus/features/time-zones.html#otheroptions"},{"t":"Using milliseconds for dates","g":"Time Zones","u":"graph-nexus/features/time-zones.html#ms"},{"t":"Asynchronous JavaScript","g":"Best Practices","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/asynchronous-javascript.html","x":"Asynchronous JavaScript Introduction # Functions can be either synchronous (sync, blocking) or asynchronous (async, non-blocking): Synchronous functions - executed one after another, the program waits for each individual statement to finish and return a result before executing the next function. Asynchronous functions - executed one after another, but the program doesn’t wait for the previous function to finish before executing the next. Asynchronous programming is often used in JavaScript applications. AJAX, DOM changes and setTimeout are all examples of common async functions. Asynchronous functions in Nexus # Async functions in Nexus are clearly labelled in the API Reference page , for example: Nexus uses async functions to make sure long-running functions don’t block other interactions. For example, if your chart contains thousands of nodes, you don’t want to wait for the layout to complete the entire computation every time you call chart.layout() . You still want to pan and zoom the chart while you’re waiting for the call to complete. These methods return a Promise, which is an object representing the state of the asynchronous method and its eventual result, and can be used to"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Asynchronous JavaScript","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/asynchronous-javascript.html#intro"},{"t":"Asynchronous functions in Nexus","g":"Asynchronous JavaScript","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/asynchronous-javascript.html#async"},{"t":"Asynchronous image loading","g":"Asynchronous JavaScript","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/asynchronous-javascript.html#imageloading"},{"t":"Using Nexus with callbacks","g":"Asynchronous JavaScript","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/asynchronous-javascript.html#callbacks"},{"t":"Coding and Testing Tips","g":"Best Practices","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/coding-and-testing-tips.html","x":"Coding and Testing Tips This section links to some useful information for both new and experienced programmers and testers. Advice from Nexus Developers # The developers who build this SDK have compiled some simple resources to help you plan, build and test your application. Planning your application # How to build great network visualizations Visualizing very large networks The Five Pitfalls of Network Visualization The 10 rules of great graph design Coding with Nexus # Five mistakes you probably make in your front-end JavaScript Asynchronous JavaScript Testing your application # Over time we’ve experimented with a wide range of different testing, benchmarking and analysis modules. As you will likely have specific requirements for testing, we cannot recommend any modules. However, here are a number of test modules that we find useful: Mocha: NodeJS Test Framework # Mocha is one of the most popular test frameworks available in NodeJS. While the framework itself is very complete it’s easy to use even when testing complex asynchronous code. Using a self-contained JS file loading in a webpage, Mocha can be used for both NodeJS testing and front-end JavaScript testing. The module also "},{"t":"Advice from Nexus Developers","g":"Coding and Testing Tips","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/coding-and-testing-tips.html#advice-from-graph-nexus-developers"},{"t":"Planning your application","g":"Coding and Testing Tips","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/coding-and-testing-tips.html#planning-your-application"},{"t":"Coding with Nexus","g":"Coding and Testing Tips","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/coding-and-testing-tips.html#coding-with-graph-nexus"},{"t":"Testing your application","g":"Coding and Testing Tips","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/coding-and-testing-tips.html#testing-your-application"},{"t":"Automated UI Testing","g":"Coding and Testing Tips","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/coding-and-testing-tips.html#automated-ui-testing"},{"t":"Cross-Origin Images (CORS)","g":"Best Practices","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/cross-origin-images-cors.html","x":"Cross-Origin Images (CORS) The W3C standard for Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) imposes security restrictions on certain Nexus functions. How does CORS affect Nexus? # If you run pages from a file system instead of a web server, CORS prevents Nexus from running in WebGL mode. Images from different origins are affected by the same-origin policy and won't appear on your chart, or be included in screenshots taken with chart.toDataURL() , or be included in the output of chart.export() . This includes images on nodes or glyphs, as well as font icons and map tiles . For example, you may want to reference an image that doesn't originate from the same domain as the Nexus library file. Your browser will mark it as insecure and prevent the image from being loaded. Nexus will attempt to load the image again with the crossorigin HTML attribute set. You can use the GraphNexus.corsAdapter() function to change the value of the attribute. If it still cannot load the image, it will mark it as missing and throw a SecurityError exception. How do I fix this? # To use Nexus in WebGL mode, you should run it from a web server, not a file system. To make sure your images display properly, do one of t"},{"t":"How does CORS affect Nexus?","g":"Cross-Origin Images (CORS)","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/cross-origin-images-cors.html#how-does-cors-affect-graph-nexus"},{"t":"How do I fix this?","g":"Cross-Origin Images (CORS)","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/cross-origin-images-cors.html#howtofix"},{"t":"Use access-control headers","g":"Cross-Origin Images (CORS)","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/cross-origin-images-cors.html#access-control"},{"t":"Proxy the resources","g":"Cross-Origin Images (CORS)","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/cross-origin-images-cors.html#proxy"},{"t":"Image Formats","g":"Best Practices","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/image-formats.html","x":"Image Formats Using images on nodes and glyphs is a powerful way to customise charts. Nexus supports the most popular image formats, including PNG, JPG and SVG, as well as font icons. SVG support # SVGs work in every browser Nexus supports. We've tested webicons and IconExperience SVGs in Canvas and WebGL against Chrome, Firefox, Microsoft Edge and Safari. Our results verified that SVGs are fully supported as long as: they don’t have foreign object tags you’ve set width and height attributes in the SVG file SVGs in WebGL # To ensure the highest level of performance and stability, SVGs are converted to bitmaps when they're drawn. Like any image, we restrict them to a maximum of 256x256px, so you’ll lose sharpness if you zoom in any further than this. For more recommendations and known issues, see WebGL . Which image format should I use? # We recommend the following: for the most reliable cross-browser support, use PNGs or JPGs. for the best scalability, and to dynamically change the colour of the item, use font icons. if it's important to have smaller file sizes, or for images of more than one colour to look good at high resolution, use SVGs."},{"t":"SVG support","g":"Image Formats","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/image-formats.html#support"},{"t":"SVGs in WebGL","g":"Image Formats","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/image-formats.html#svg"},{"t":"Which image format should I use?","g":"Image Formats","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/image-formats.html#formats"},{"t":"Obfuscation Requirements","g":"Best Practices","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/obfuscation-requirements.html","x":"Obfuscation Requirements Obscuring Nexus source code # Nexus source code must be obscured within your application. To prevent it being accessed or reused by third parties you need to: Exclude the words 'Nexus' or 'Graph Nexus' from any folder or path names. Combine the Nexus files with the source code from your application, so that Nexus is not available as a separate file. We recommend using Terser with any of the following bundlers (any tool which achieves the results listed is acceptable): Webpack Parcel Rollup Remove the copyright notice (this is usually a minification step). Ensure you are not deploying source maps of your source code to your end users. Fully integrate Nexus into your product. We don't allow direct exposure of the Nexus API to end users via your own API ‘wrapper’. Before deployment # Before you deploy your code, you also need to check that: The words 'Nexus' or 'Graph Nexus' aren’t in any folder or path names. The final file containing Nexus is at least 20% larger than the original Nexus library file. There are no Graph Nexus copyright notices in your deployed code bundles."},{"t":"Obscuring Nexus source code","g":"Obfuscation Requirements","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/obfuscation-requirements.html#obscuring-graph-nexus"},{"t":"Before deployment","g":"Obfuscation Requirements","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/obfuscation-requirements.html#before-deployment"},{"t":"Resizing Components","g":"Best Practices","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/resizing-components.html","x":"Resizing Components Introduction # Nexus chart and time bar components adapt to fit the size of their parent element in the DOM. Any changes in the size of the parent will be adopted by the chart or time bar, making it easy to manipulate their size and shape. The Nexus container # The GraphNexus.create() function creates a container that the chart sits inside. So when you create a chart or time bar, that element will be a child of the specified container. Your html element structure should then look something like this: Note that a Nexus component will not render to a container if the container's css style property has display: none , width = 0 or height = 0 . Styling and CSS # As the chart resizes automatically you should make sure you target your CSS at the container, and not the Nexus element itself. As Nexus elements adapt to fill the size of their parent, we advise using box-sizing to make sizing the chart simpler: Nexus elements adapt to fit the size of their parent, so you should be aware of other rules in css modules that may interfere with this. Modules such as css grid - where parent elements are sized based on their children - can cause unexpected behaviour. Fullscreen m"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Resizing Components","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/resizing-components.html#intro"},{"t":"The Nexus container","g":"Resizing Components","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/resizing-components.html#create"},{"t":"Styling and CSS","g":"Resizing Components","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/resizing-components.html#styling"},{"t":"Fullscreen mode","g":"Resizing Components","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/resizing-components.html#fullscreen"},{"t":"Resizing in older Nexus versions","g":"Resizing Components","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/resizing-components.html#before5-3"},{"t":"Troubleshooting","g":"Best Practices","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/troubleshooting.html","x":"Troubleshooting General troubleshooting tips # Before contacting Nexus Support about an issue, you may want to consider the following:- What version of Nexus are you using? Is it the latest? Are you running in WebGL or Canvas mode? You can find the answers to these questions using the GraphNexus.info() function, and it's worth having this information to hand if you do contact Support. How can I stop Nexus being blurry? # If you change the size of the Nexus component, you should use the GraphNexus.setSize function. Do not set the width/height of the component yourself via the DOM or jQuery, because otherwise the component will not be scaled correctly. My chart has gone blank - what might be causing this? # Most often the container has no size. The chart fills its container element, so give the container an explicit height (for example in CSS) and use GraphNexus.setSize() if you resize it from code. Also check the developer tools console for errors, and call GraphNexus.info() to confirm which version and rendering mode are in use. Why do my SVG icons fail to load in some browsers? # If some browsers fail to load your SVG icons while others work correctly, it's likely a browser issue."},{"t":"General troubleshooting tips","g":"Troubleshooting","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/troubleshooting.html#tips"},{"t":"How can I stop Nexus being blurry?","g":"Troubleshooting","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/troubleshooting.html#blurry"},{"t":"My chart has gone blank - what might be causing this?","g":"Troubleshooting","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/troubleshooting.html#expiry"},{"t":"Why do my SVG icons fail to load in some browsers?","g":"Troubleshooting","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/troubleshooting.html#svgload"},{"t":"General browser issues","g":"Troubleshooting","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/troubleshooting.html#general"},{"t":"Firefox","g":"Troubleshooting","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/troubleshooting.html#firefox"},{"t":"How do I stop nodes moving around when I add data?","g":"Troubleshooting","u":"graph-nexus/best-practices/troubleshooting.html#addingNodes"},{"t":"Angular Reference","g":"Third Party Integrations","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/angular-reference.html","x":"Angular Reference New to Nexus with Angular? Start with the Angular tutorial . Components # Standalone components for Angular 15 and later (an optional peer dependency of graph-nexus ). Add them to a component's imports . Each renders a div that fills the host element, so give the host a size (it is an inline element by default; set display: block ). The Nexus component is created after view init and destroyed on destroy. Inputs # Input Type Description data ChartData / { items: TimeBarItem[] } Loaded after creation and reloaded with load() on change. options ChartOptions / TimeBarOptions Passed to GraphNexus.create() and re-applied with options() on change. Outputs # Output Payload Description ready Chart / TimeBar Emitted once after the initial data has loaded. graphEvent { name, event } Emitted for every chart or time bar event. See Chart Events and Time Bar Events . The components do not lay out data automatically; call chart.layout() in the ready handler, or supply positions in the data."},{"t":"Components","g":"Angular Reference","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/angular-reference.html#components"},{"t":"Inputs","g":"Angular Reference","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/angular-reference.html#inputs"},{"t":"Outputs","g":"Angular Reference","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/angular-reference.html#outputs"},{"t":"Neo4j Tutorial","g":"Third Party Integrations","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/neo4j-tutorial.html","x":"Neo4j Tutorial Note: the Neo4j demo source files this tutorial refers to are not shipped with Nexus. Use the steps as a guide for wiring the Neo4j JavaScript driver into your own app. Introduction # This guide will help you integrate Nexus with Neo4j, one of many popular databases you can use with Nexus. This tutorial uses the Neo4j Javascript driver to connect a simple Nexus application to a Neo4j database. The driver uses the Bolt protocol to connect to the database, and has three advantages over using a REST API/HTTP protocol: Speed – binary encoding means less data gets sent over the wire, and access is faster. Performance – using Websockets, high volumes of queries are executed more quickly, giving higher throughput and lower latency. Security – Bolt has TLS security built and enabled by default in to protect the data connection. For more information on how you can load your formatted data into Nexus, see also Loading Data . The tutorial has been tested and verified with version 1.5.7 of Neo4j Desktop and version 5.5.0 of Neo4j. While other versions of Neo4j Desktop and Neo4j v5 may also work, they haven't been tested and verified as working. Step 1: Setting up the environment"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Neo4j Tutorial","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/neo4j-tutorial.html#intro"},{"t":"Step 1: Setting up the environment","g":"Neo4j Tutorial","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/neo4j-tutorial.html#step1"},{"t":"Step 2: Creating a Neo4j database","g":"Neo4j Tutorial","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/neo4j-tutorial.html#step2"},{"t":"Step 3: Connecting Nexus to the database","g":"Neo4j Tutorial","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/neo4j-tutorial.html#step3"},{"t":"Understanding the application architecture","g":"Neo4j Tutorial","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/neo4j-tutorial.html#architecture"},{"t":"React Reference","g":"Third Party Integrations","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/react-reference.html","x":"React Reference New to Nexus with React? Start with the React tutorial . Components # Both are function components built on hooks. They need React 17 or later (an optional peer dependency of graph-nexus ). Each renders a div that fills its parent ( width and height 100%) and creates the Nexus component inside it on mount; it is destroyed on unmount. Props # Prop Type Description data object Data in Nexus format, loaded on creation and reloaded with load() whenever the object reference changes. options object Passed to GraphNexus.create() and re-applied with options() whenever the reference changes. on object Event name to handler, for example { click: e => {} } . Every chart or time bar event is supported; the latest object is used on each event, so inline handlers are fine. onReady function Called once with the Nexus chart or time bar after the initial data has loaded. Keep a reference to call the API. className string Class for the container div . style object Inline style merged over { width: '100%', height: '100%' } . The components do not lay out data automatically; call chart.layout() in onReady , or supply positions in the data. Calling the API # For events and methods see C"},{"t":"Components","g":"React Reference","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/react-reference.html#components"},{"t":"Props","g":"React Reference","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/react-reference.html#props"},{"t":"Calling the API","g":"React Reference","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/react-reference.html#api"},{"t":"Platforms and Dependencies","g":"Third Party Integrations","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/third-party-libraries.html","x":"Platforms and Dependencies Browser compatibility, runtime dependencies, and licenses for Nexus deployments. Platform support # For the current list of supported devices, operating systems, and browsers, see Compatibility and Supported Platforms. Runtime dependencies # Nexus has no required third-party runtime dependencies. It does not extend built-in JavaScript types and keeps its API within its own namespace to avoid global conflicts. Two optional features require additional libraries: Map mode uses Leaflet ; OpenStreetMap tiles are used by default. PDF export uses PDFKit and can also use SVG-to-PDFKit . Documentation site licenses # However, this website uses various common client side libraries for its functionality: Angular - MIT License Babel - MIT License Esri Leaflet - MIT License Esri Leaflet Geocoder - Apache License Font Awesome - Free and Pro Fuse.js - Apache License Gremlin - MIT License jQuery - MIT License jQuery UI - MIT License Leaflet 1.9.1 - BSD-2-Clause Leaflet.heat - BSD-2-Clause Leaflet PiP - BSD-2-Clause Mustache - MIT License OpenStreetMap - ODbL License PDFKit - MIT License Prettify - Apache License React - MIT License Reflect Metadata - Apache License Super"},{"t":"Platform support","g":"Platforms and Dependencies","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/third-party-libraries.html#platform-support"},{"t":"Runtime dependencies","g":"Platforms and Dependencies","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/third-party-libraries.html#runtime-dependencies"},{"t":"Documentation site licenses","g":"Platforms and Dependencies","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/third-party-libraries.html#documentation-site-licenses"},{"t":"Vue Reference","g":"Third Party Integrations","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/vue-reference.html","x":"Vue Reference New to Nexus with Vue? Start with the Vue tutorial . Components # Vue 3 components (Vue 3 is an optional peer dependency of graph-nexus ). Each renders a div that fills its parent, creates the Nexus component on mount and destroys it before unmount. Props # Prop Type Default Description data object null Data in Nexus format, loaded on mount and reloaded with load() when the prop changes. options object null Passed to GraphNexus.create() and re-applied with options() on any (deep) change. Events # Event Description ready Emitted once with the Nexus chart or time bar after the initial data has loaded. any chart / time bar event Re-emitted under its own name with the event object, for example @click , @double-click , @selection-change . See Chart Events and Time Bar Events . graph-event Emitted for every event as { name, event } . The components do not lay out data automatically; call chart.layout() in the ready handler, or supply positions in the data. Calling the API # Keep the object from @ready , or use a template ref: the component exposes component() , which returns the Nexus chart or time bar (or null before it is ready)."},{"t":"Components","g":"Vue Reference","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/vue-reference.html#components"},{"t":"Props","g":"Vue Reference","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/vue-reference.html#props"},{"t":"Events","g":"Vue Reference","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/vue-reference.html#events"},{"t":"Calling the API","g":"Vue Reference","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/vue-reference.html#api"},{"t":"WebGL","g":"Third Party Integrations","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/webgl.html","x":"WebGL What is WebGL? How to check if your environment is supported How to use WebGL in Nexus Why use WebGL? Supported Environments Security SVGs Recommendations Limitations and Known Issues Support What is WebGL? # WebGL (Web Graphics Library) is an alternative rendering method to HTML5 Canvas. It delivers high performance rendering in compatible web browsers without using plug-ins. It's supported in leading browsers and many hardware platforms, including mobile devices, where it exploits the power of Graphics Processing Units (GPUs). WebGL is designed and maintained by the non-profit Khronos Group . How to check if your environment supports WebGL # To check if your device meets the minimum requirements for WebGL, run GraphNexus.webGLSupport() . How to use WebGL in Nexus # Nexus 3.5 and later use WebGL by default. You'll need to enable hardware acceleration in your browser settings. If WebGL is disabled, Nexus uses Canvas instead. To switch off WebGL, before you create a chart, call GraphNexus.mode(\"canvas\") . Why use WebGL? # Performance. The faster the visualisation is updated, the more responsive and productive the experience is. Your users can analyse large charts and datasets "},{"t":"What is WebGL?","g":"WebGL","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/webgl.html#what"},{"t":"How to check if your environment supports WebGL","g":"WebGL","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/webgl.html#howtocheck"},{"t":"How to use WebGL in Nexus","g":"WebGL","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/webgl.html#howtouse"},{"t":"Why use WebGL?","g":"WebGL","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/webgl.html#why"},{"t":"Supported Environments","g":"WebGL","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/webgl.html#supportedenv"},{"t":"Security","g":"WebGL","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/webgl.html#security"},{"t":"SVGs","g":"WebGL","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/webgl.html#svgs"},{"t":"Recommendations","g":"WebGL","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/webgl.html#recommendations"},{"t":"Limitations and Known Issues","g":"WebGL","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/webgl.html#knownissues"},{"t":"Support","g":"WebGL","u":"graph-nexus/integrations/webgl.html#support"},{"t":"Accessibility","g":"About Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/about/accessibility.html","x":"Accessibility Nexus is a visual tool, so we don't support many of the accessibility requirements for non-visual representations described in W3C’s Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.0 and Section 508 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 . Having said that, we work hard to make sure you can create applications that are as accessible and easy to use as possible. Here are some guidelines. Browser and operating system support # Operating systems and browsers have built-in accessibility features such as screen magnifiers, keyboard shortcuts and system speech packages. Check their accessibility documentation to see what comes for free. For example: Apple: Accessibility Support Firefox: Accessibility Features Google: Android Accessibility Overview Using a keyboard instead of a mouse # Nexus supports the following shortcuts by default, although some keys may differ depending on your keyboard: Action Shortcut Cancel and revert active dragging Esc Delete selected items Del Move selected items Up, down, left, right arrows Play timebar animation Space Select all consistentCtrl + A Some standard mouse-free navigation is supported by your browser, such as using the TAB key to cycle throu"},{"t":"Browser and operating system support","g":"Accessibility","u":"graph-nexus/about/accessibility.html#browser-and-os"},{"t":"Using a keyboard instead of a mouse","g":"Accessibility","u":"graph-nexus/about/accessibility.html#keyboard"},{"t":"Creating text versions of charts","g":"Accessibility","u":"graph-nexus/about/accessibility.html#text-charts"},{"t":"Working with audio","g":"Accessibility","u":"graph-nexus/about/accessibility.html#audio"},{"t":"Choosing colours","g":"Accessibility","u":"graph-nexus/about/accessibility.html#colours"},{"t":"Using ping with caution","g":"Accessibility","u":"graph-nexus/about/accessibility.html#ping"},{"t":"Alpha/Beta Features","g":"About Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/about/alpha-beta-features.html","x":"Alpha/Beta Features We’re constantly developing features to improve Nexus. Before they become an established part of the Nexus toolkit, they're labelled as Alpha or Beta. Alpha # Alpha features have been fully tested. This is a developmental stage, so we reserve the right to introduce breaking changes to the API. These will be documented fully in Breaking Changes. If there are known limitations or usage restrictions, we’ll highlight them in the Release Notes. We’re keen for you to use these features, but you should not deploy them to production. We do not support Alpha features in a production environment. Beta # Beta features have been fully tested. Some Alpha features become Beta features once they’ve been developed further based on user feedback. Some Beta features don’t go through Alpha because they’re already at an advanced stage of development. This is still a developmental stage, so we reserve the right to introduce breaking changes to the API. These will be documented fully in Breaking Changes. It’s safe to deploy Beta features to production , but remember that breaking changes will affect your production upgrades. Once we’ve made further improvements to Beta features and g"},{"t":"Alpha","g":"Alpha/Beta Features","u":"graph-nexus/about/alpha-beta-features.html#alpha"},{"t":"Beta","g":"Alpha/Beta Features","u":"graph-nexus/about/alpha-beta-features.html#beta"},{"t":"Share your feedback","g":"Alpha/Beta Features","u":"graph-nexus/about/alpha-beta-features.html#feedback"},{"t":"Learn about changes","g":"Alpha/Beta Features","u":"graph-nexus/about/alpha-beta-features.html#changes"},{"t":"Support","g":"Alpha/Beta Features","u":"graph-nexus/about/alpha-beta-features.html#support"},{"t":"Deprecation Process","g":"About Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/about/deprecation-process.html","x":"Deprecation Process When we announce that support for an API, platform, database or framework is deprecated, it means that we intend to end support for it in future. We recommend that you stop using it now. Deprecations of APIs are rare and only happen in exceptional circumstances, for example: there's no longer a use case for it it's been replaced by something better it was an experimental feature that we've stopped developing the support costs outweigh the benefits to users We may also deprecate support for platforms, databases or frameworks for similar reasons. How the process works # Initial announcement We'll add details of what's being deprecated to the current list of deprecations. Deprecated items will only become unsupported in a future major release version, but we'll always give you at least six months notice before a deprecated item becomes unsupported. We'll also tag deprecated items in the API Reference . If we plan to end support for a certain platform, we'll confirm this by email at least six months in advance. Opportunity for you to make changes and send feedback If there's an item on the list that's in your current codebase, you should remove it as soon as possibl"},{"t":"How the process works","g":"Deprecation Process","u":"graph-nexus/about/deprecation-process.html#process"},{"t":"Performance","g":"About Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/about/performance.html","x":"Performance Introduction # There are a number of ways that you can improve the performance of your Nexus application. Poor performance can usually be resolved with good design, so here are a few common solutions that you can try. Reducing the amount of Data # Nexus can display thousands of nodes and links, but it's rarely a good idea to push Nexus to it's limits. Not only will performance deteriorate with lots of items, but users will find networks harder to understand. Having fewer items not only improves rendering times, but also improves the performance of functions, such as layouts, which involve complex calculations on visible items. Filtering # Filtering is a great way to reduce the amount of data that you need to process, and can be done within Nexus as well as in your back-end environment. Nexus' built-in filter function lets you only show items that match a certain criteria, stopping you from overloading users with information. Less visible data will also speed up chart functions such as layouts. Combos # Another way to reduce the amount of visible data is to use Combos to group nodes together. Combos are a powerful feature that hide data initially, while allowing users to"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Performance","u":"graph-nexus/about/performance.html#intro"},{"t":"Reducing the amount of Data","g":"Performance","u":"graph-nexus/about/performance.html#data"},{"t":"Rendering Mode","g":"Performance","u":"graph-nexus/about/performance.html#render"},{"t":"Data manipulation","g":"Performance","u":"graph-nexus/about/performance.html#changingdata"},{"t":"Asynchronicity","g":"Performance","u":"graph-nexus/about/performance.html#async"},{"t":"Browsers","g":"Performance","u":"graph-nexus/about/performance.html#browsers"},{"t":"Support","g":"Performance","u":"graph-nexus/about/performance.html#support"},{"t":"Security","g":"About Nexus","u":"graph-nexus/about/security.html","x":"Security Web application developers are serious about security. They work hard to maintain secure environments and minimize the risk of attack. But there are still security violations, and without due diligence it’s possible to expose serious vulnerabilities. For more information, see the Open Web Application Security Project’s (OWASP) Ten Most Critical Web Application Security Risks . Nexus Is Inherently Secure # Nexus is a low-risk, highly secure JavaScript library. It is unlikely to be affected by common security vulnerabilities because: There are no third-party dependencies in the core library. Nexus code was written by the Graph Nexus team.* Nexus does not natively send data to remote servers or depend on server-side components. Internal data structures are not available through the global scope or public interface. Nexus does not track user data or persist data in any form of local storage. Source code is obfuscated and minified before distribution, so it’s difficult for malicious agents to hijack. Nexus runs wholly within the browser using standard JavaScript. Browsers are frequently patched to reduce the likelihood of security breaches. In addition, new APIs and features ar"},{"t":"Nexus Is Inherently Secure","g":"Security","u":"graph-nexus/about/security.html#secure"},{"t":"Automated Tooling","g":"Security","u":"graph-nexus/about/security.html#autotool"},{"t":"Policy Review","g":"Security","u":"graph-nexus/about/security.html#policy"},{"t":"Timeline (Plain JS)","g":"Timeline · Overview","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/docs.html","x":"Timeline (Plain JS) The Timeline is the timeline visualization component included in Graph Nexus. These guides cover installation, entities and events, time handling, styling and integration with Nexus. Timeline · Overview # API Reference Timeline · Getting Started # Getting Started Updating Timeline · Basics # Timeline Essentials Scales Timeline · Items # Entities Events Markers Time Series Charts Heatmap Font Icons Timeline · Data and Time # Handling Data Updating Data Handling Time Time Zones Timeline · Interaction # Handling Events Timeline · Integration # Nexus Integration Export Migration Timeline · About # Architecture Accessibility Obfuscation Requirements Alpha, Beta and Deprecated Features"},{"t":"Timeline · Overview","g":"Timeline (Plain JS)","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/docs.html#timeline-overview"},{"t":"Timeline · Getting Started","g":"Timeline (Plain JS)","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/docs.html#timeline-getting-started"},{"t":"Timeline · Basics","g":"Timeline (Plain JS)","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/docs.html#timeline-basics"},{"t":"Timeline · Items","g":"Timeline (Plain JS)","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/docs.html#timeline-items"},{"t":"Timeline · Data and Time","g":"Timeline (Plain JS)","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/docs.html#timeline-data-and-time"},{"t":"Timeline · Interaction","g":"Timeline (Plain JS)","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/docs.html#timeline-interaction"},{"t":"Timeline · Integration","g":"Timeline (Plain JS)","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/docs.html#timeline-integration"},{"t":"Timeline · About","g":"Timeline (Plain JS)","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/docs.html#timeline-about"},{"t":"Nexus Timeline API Reference","g":"Timeline · Overview","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/api.html","x":"Nexus Timeline API Reference Creating a Timeline # createTimeline(componentDefinition) Creates a Nexus Timeline timeline in your web page within the specified DOM element. The timeline sets its height and width to equal the size of its parent element, or 100px if the parent does not have these. We recommend styling the parent of the timeline, not the timeline itself. Parameters componentDefinition object | string required Specifies values for the timeline to be created, or the id of the container element in which to create the timeline. container HTMLElement | string required The mounted DOM element in which to insert the component, or its id. options TimelineOptions Options for the new component. Returns Timeline The created Timeline object. When new or updated data is passed in, it will redraw with the new items. Timeline Methods # destroy() Destroys the current instance of the timeline, freeing any allocated resources. Parameters none Returns void export(options) async Exports a static image of the timeline. The exported image is encoded in a blob URL. Parameters options ExportOptions required Options related to producing an export of the current view. type 'png' required The fi"},{"t":"Creating a Timeline","g":"Nexus Timeline API Reference","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/api.html#createTimeline"},{"t":"Timeline Methods","g":"Nexus Timeline API Reference","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/api.html#timeline-methods"},{"t":"Handling Events","g":"Nexus Timeline API Reference","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/api.html#handling-events"},{"t":"Timeline Options","g":"Nexus Timeline API Reference","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/api.html#timeline-options"},{"t":"Data Formats","g":"Nexus Timeline API Reference","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/api.html#data-formats"},{"t":"Color Formats","g":"Nexus Timeline API Reference","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/api.html#ColorFormats"},{"t":"Getting Started","g":"Timeline · Getting Started","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/getting-started.html","x":"Getting Started Introduction # Nexus Timeline is a JavaScript library for building time-based visualizations. This guide introduces some of the basic principles of building a timeline to get you up-and-running with Nexus Timeline. If you are looking for the React version, use the toggle above. We are going to embed a simple timeline in an HTML page, before adding data and attaching an event handler. Create and Serve an App # First we need to set up the file structure inside your project's root folder. You can also use an existing app, but your architecture may require you to make some changes. Copy the following into package.json . We are going to use Parcel to bundle and serve the app, but you can use an alternative. Then install the dependencies and start the server: Or if you're using npm: Some browsers cache aggressively, so during development you may need to manually refresh. If you are using TypeScript, then you can include your index.ts file in your index.html page and Parcel will automatically transpile it for you. Installing # Install Graph Nexus from your package registry or from a packed tarball built with npm run build && npm pack : Creating a Timeline # It's time to ad"},{"t":"Introduction","g":"Getting Started","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/getting-started.html#introduction"},{"t":"Create and Serve an App","g":"Getting Started","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/getting-started.html#create-and-serve"},{"t":"Installing","g":"Getting Started","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/getting-started.html#installing"},{"t":"Creating a Timeline","g":"Getting Started","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/getting-started.html#creating-a-timeline"},{"t":"Handling Interactions","g":"Getting Started","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/getting-started.html#handling-interactions"},{"t":"Updating","g":"Timeline · Getting Started","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/updating.html","x":"Updating Update Timeline together with Graph Nexus using your package manager: Verify the installed version with npm list graph-nexus or yarn list --pattern graph-nexus ."},{"t":"Timeline Essentials","g":"Timeline · Basics","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/timeline-basics.html","x":"Timeline Essentials Items # Nexus Timeline is a JavaScript library that lets you add Timeline components to your web applications. Timeline components show the timelines of entities and linked events, creating time-based data visualizations. Entities are persistent actors or objects with horizontally drawn timelines. Entities can be for example people, phone numbers or bank accounts, and they can have a type to define their styling and grouping behavior. Interactions # Horizontal drag or scroll to pan the timeline. Vertical scroll to zoom the timeline. Drag + shift to zoom to a marquee range. Customization # Use timeline options to adapt appearance and interaction to your data and workflow. Options # Nexus Timeline allows you to set up the timeline in an almost infinite number of ways. Which means you can change the look, feel and behavior in a way that suits your requirements and data. Most settings can be found in TimelineOptions . Pass them to the options method to make the changes. See the Timeline Options reference for the full list. This is the default timeline. If no timeline options are defined then the default values will apply. Show Lens Highlight # By default, when the l"},{"t":"Items","g":"Timeline Essentials","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/timeline-basics.html#items"},{"t":"Interactions","g":"Timeline Essentials","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/timeline-basics.html#interactions"},{"t":"Customization","g":"Timeline Essentials","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/timeline-basics.html#customization"},{"t":"Options","g":"Timeline Essentials","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/timeline-basics.html#options"},{"t":"Show Lens Highlight","g":"Timeline Essentials","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/timeline-basics.html#show-lens-highlight"},{"t":"Label Area Width","g":"Timeline Essentials","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/timeline-basics.html#label-area-width"},{"t":"Scales","g":"Timeline · Basics","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/scales.html","x":"Scales Scale Localization # Dates and times are displayed in the scale in US English format by default. You can use the dateOrder and twelveHourClock options for basic adjustment of the US English format. For further customization of the displayed date and time you can use: dateTimeFormats for different scale zoom levels and wrapping options. Each one is a string containing formatting codes and/or literal characters. dateTimeNames for months and days of the week, as well as the AM/PM symbols. Format codes: f through fffffffff select one through nine fractional-second digits, while t and tt select the localized short and full AM/PM names. You can see some examples of date and time formatting for different locales in the Scales Options story. Scale Wrapping # By default, time flows along the timeline component in a linear way, showing events in the sequence that they occurred. Nexus Timeline offers an alternative view of time using scale wrapping, which positions events based on part of their timestamp, such as the hour of the day or the day of the week. This lets you visualize repeating patterns within the data, making it clearer how activity is distributed. This is also sometimes c"},{"t":"Scale Localization","g":"Scales","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/scales.html#scale-localization"},{"t":"Scale Wrapping","g":"Scales","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/scales.html#scale-wrapping"},{"t":"Entities","g":"Timeline · Items","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/entities.html","x":"Entities Entity Styling # Entities run horizontally on the timeline and are shown when they have an associated event in the visible time range. Customize and style your entities with the properties in the Entity object. Here the first entity has all the default properties and the second entity has some custom properties set: Try out some of the properties in the Styling Entities story. Entity Types # If you have a number of entities that you want to customize in the same way, use the EntityType object and then assign a type to the Entity . Each entity can only have one type. In these examples, we are using a subset of the \"Bigfoot Sightings\" data from the Bigfoot Field Researchers Organization (BFRO). Here, the numbered sightings are organized into types by State. Each State has its own EntityType where the entity color is set. This is how the entityTypes are assigned to the entities using the type property: See types in use in the Styling Items by Type story. Entity Base Types # Entity types can inherit properties from a baseType . In this example, each entity has a type based on its classification. \"Class A\" and \"Class B\" set the labelColor and the entity color is inherited from "},{"t":"Entity Styling","g":"Entities","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/entities.html#entity-styling"},{"t":"Entity Types","g":"Entities","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/entities.html#entity-types"},{"t":"Entity Base Types","g":"Entities","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/entities.html#entity-base-types"},{"t":"Default Entity Type","g":"Entities","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/entities.html#default-entity-type"},{"t":"Entity Groups","g":"Entities","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/entities.html#entity-groups"},{"t":"Glyphs","g":"Entities","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/entities.html#glyphs"},{"t":"Events","g":"Timeline · Items","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/events.html","x":"Events Event Styling # Events can be styled and customized with the properties in the Event object. In this example the color and lineWidth properties have been changed for \"event2\". See the Styling Events story for more examples. Event Types # If you have a number of events that you want to customize in the same way, use the EventType object and then assign a type to the Event . In these examples we are using a subset of the \"Street Network Changes\" from the City of New York. Here, the street change events are styled by the type of change. Each type of change has its own EventType where a color and fontIcon are added. This is how the EventTypes are assigned to the events using the type property: Event Base Types # Properties can be inherited using a baseType . In this example each event's color is derived from its type . The fontIcon is set on the baseType of each type. See the Inherited Styles story for more details. Default Event Type # Events that have unspecified properties will inherit them from the default type. Assign properties to the default event type as follows: Note the default type cannot have a baseType . Event Summaries # For event joins, i.e. the points where event"},{"t":"Event Styling","g":"Events","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/events.html#event-styling"},{"t":"Event Types","g":"Events","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/events.html#event-types"},{"t":"Event Base Types","g":"Events","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/events.html#event-base-types"},{"t":"Default Event Type","g":"Events","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/events.html#default-event-type"},{"t":"Event Summaries","g":"Events","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/events.html#event-summaries"},{"t":"Event Folds","g":"Events","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/events.html#event-folds"},{"t":"Markers","g":"Timeline · Items","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/markers.html","x":"Markers Markers allow you to flag a specific time or time range with a line that vertically crosses all the visible entities on the timeline. Add markers to your timeline by using the timeline.markers API. You can add as many markers to your timeline as you like. By default, markers inherit their colors and text size from the scales options. However, markers can be individually styled by specifying color , fontIcon or labelColor and other options in the Marker object. See more in the Markers story."},{"t":"Time Series Charts","g":"Timeline · Items","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/time-series-charts.html","x":"Time Series Charts Time Series Charts # Time series charts are used to visualize continuous time series data over a recorded period. You can add one or more time series charts to the timeline. Time series charts share the timeline's scale, aligning the data points with the events displayed in the timeline. This lets you identify interesting points or patterns in the data and investigate the particular events that may be related to them. This example uses a sample of the Gridwatch dataset, which tracks the sources of energy production in the UK. Set the size and position of the timeline in the timeSeriesCharts options: Note that if scale wrapping is applied, time series charts will be hidden. Styling # Time series charts can be styled and customized using the properties in the TimeSeriesChart object. Take a look at how the styles can be applied in the Time Series Charts story. Stacks # If you have more than one time series dataset, the charts can be added to a time series chart stack. This allows you to compare two related time series datasets or save space on the timeline. In this example, the energy types are grouped into 'Renewable' and 'Non-Renewable'. To stack your time series "},{"t":"Time Series Charts","g":"Time Series Charts","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/time-series-charts.html#tsc-styling"},{"t":"Stacks","g":"Time Series Charts","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/time-series-charts.html#stacks"},{"t":"Data Points","g":"Time Series Charts","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/time-series-charts.html#data-points"},{"t":"Heatmap","g":"Timeline · Items","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/heatmap.html","x":"Heatmap Heatmap # To help with visual analysis of large datasets, Nexus Timeline offers the option to aggregate individual events into a heatmap. In the heatmap view, individual cells span periods of time in the timeline. By default, the heatmap cell colors are based on the colors of underlying events and the cell color alpha values (i.e. transparency) represent the event count. Customize your heatmaps using Heatmap Values and Heatmap Colors . The heatmap view shows when there are more than 100 events in the active range. You can change the default number of events with the heatmapThreshold option. The heatmapDirection option allows you to control how the heatmap shows entity and event connections. Three options are provided: any (the default), from and to . Additional options to style the heatmap include heatmapPadding and showLines . Both can be seen in the Styling the Timeline story. For more examples, see the Heatmap section in our storybook. Heatmap Values # You can set a custom value to be used by the heatmap instead of the event count to determine the color of the cells. The custom value is defined in the Event object: Heatmap Colors # Nexus Timeline also provides heatmapCol"},{"t":"Heatmap","g":"Heatmap","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/heatmap.html#heatmap"},{"t":"Heatmap Values","g":"Heatmap","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/heatmap.html#heatmap-values"},{"t":"Heatmap Colors","g":"Heatmap","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/heatmap.html#heatmap-colors"},{"t":"Reveal Events","g":"Heatmap","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/heatmap.html#reveal-events"},{"t":"Heatmap Cells","g":"Heatmap","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/heatmap.html#heatmap-cells"},{"t":"Font Icons","g":"Timeline · Items","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/font-icons.html","x":"Font Icons Font Icons # Nexus Timeline allows you to display font icons on entities, events, glyphs and markers. Font icons on events are only displayed at zoom levels where the individual events in the timeline are not drawn too closely to one another. You can set a font family on each individual icon with the fontFamily property. Use escaped character code values in the text property to set font icons: See the Font Icons story for more details. Loading Font Icons # Fonts must be loaded into the page before they can be used in Nexus Timeline. To ensure that Nexus Timeline is able to render your font icons, wait for them to load before calling set() . There are multiple ways to control font loading, including using dedicated libraries such as Web Font Loader. Modern browsers also support the native fonts.load() function:"},{"t":"Font Icons","g":"Font Icons","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/font-icons.html#font-icons"},{"t":"Loading Font Icons","g":"Font Icons","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/font-icons.html#loading-font-icons"},{"t":"Handling Data","g":"Timeline · Data and Time","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/handling-data.html","x":"Handling Data In order to display your data in Nexus Timeline, first it needs to be transformed into JSON format. This example shows how to convert a CSV file to the correct JSON format, but a similar method can be applied to whichever format your original data is in. In our example, we're using a subset of the \"What's on the menu?\" data from the New York Public Library. Download the CSV here. The code below uses the NPM packages csvtojson and fs-extra to help with transforming and saving the appropriate file. This is the result of the transformation - a JSON file. Once the JSON file is correctly formatted it can be used to create a timeline:"},{"t":"Updating Data","g":"Timeline · Data and Time","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/updating-data.html","x":"Updating Data Nexus Timeline must decide if any data has changed every time the API is called and make the respective updates. The Timeline assumes that whenever a new object is passed into a property, something has changed and Nexus Timeline will redraw. If the same object is passed into set() , it assumes nothing has changed, even if the object itself has been altered. By relying on referential equality to detect changes, Nexus Timeline can quickly and efficiently respond to re-renders, even in very large data sets. This means that applications must be disciplined in how state is updated to prevent unnecessary redraws being called. For example, passing a new object with identical property values into the entities property will trigger the data to be reloaded and redrawn. This holds true across the state tree: if a sub property has changed, a whole new object hierarchy must be created and passed into the relevant property. Consider the following object in the entities property: To change John Smith's fontIcon.text property, we can't simply mutate the first object and pass the same into the entities property, as the change will not be recognized and our timeline will stay the same."},{"t":"Handling Time","g":"Timeline · Data and Time","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/handling-time.html","x":"Handling Time Handling Time # There are a number of formats to represent time, which means that each dataset you load into Nexus Timeline may need to be handled differently. You will need to convert your time into a Date object or a millisecond number. Standard JavaScript handles time down to millisecond resolution, which is one thousandth of a second. Nexus Timeline will always return dates as Date objects. You can then convert them into the format that suits you best. Higher Resolution Time # For the majority of applications, milliseconds show sufficient time resolution to display your data. However, in some cases, milliseconds just aren't granular enough. Nexus Timeline allows for the use of nanoseconds (1/1,000,000,000 of a second, or 10 -9 seconds) by accepting and returning an extra value in addition to standard date values to account for these smaller fractions of seconds. Depending on how time is recorded in your data, it is possible to break date strings into a Date object and a number of nanoseconds, shown in the following example: See the MDN docs for more information about manipulating strings. You can then assign the values to the appropriate time properties, in this c"},{"t":"Handling Time","g":"Handling Time","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/handling-time.html#handling-time"},{"t":"Higher Resolution Time","g":"Handling Time","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/handling-time.html#higher-resolution-time"},{"t":"Range Limits","g":"Handling Time","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/handling-time.html#range-limits"},{"t":"Time Zones","g":"Timeline · Data and Time","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/time-zones.html","x":"Time Zones Time Zones # Every geographical region has its own standard time zone. Each zone has a UTC offset, which is the difference in hours and minutes from Universal Coordinated Time (UTC). Many regions observe Daylight Saving Time (DST), where the offset changes during the seasons. Offset rules may also change for unpredictable political reasons. JavaScript has no built-in functionality to manage multiple time zones, but it does have functionality to map between UTC and the user's local time zone, defined by the operating system. The JavaScript Date constructor treats most input formats as local time. The exceptions are single dateStrings with a declared time zone and millisecond numbers, which use UTC. See the MDN Docs for more details. If you are using timeNanoseconds , the value will remain the same regardless of the time zone. Time Zones in Nexus Timeline # Nexus Timeline also has no built-in functionality to manage time zones, and will display dates exactly as they're passed in. You will need to decide which time zone to use for Nexus Timeline events. The most common choices are either the user's time zone or UTC. User Time Conversions # Sometimes users want to see dates "},{"t":"Time Zones","g":"Time Zones","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/time-zones.html#time-zones"},{"t":"Time Zones in Nexus Timeline","g":"Time Zones","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/time-zones.html#time-zones-in-nexus-timelinegraph"},{"t":"User Time Conversions","g":"Time Zones","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/time-zones.html#user-time-conversions"},{"t":"Server-side Conversions","g":"Time Zones","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/time-zones.html#server-side-conversions"},{"t":"Millisecond Conversions","g":"Time Zones","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/time-zones.html#millisecond-conversions"},{"t":"Handling Events","g":"Timeline · Interaction","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/handling-events.html","x":"Handling Events Handling Events # Nexus Timeline lets you respond to different kinds of events fired by the timeline: user interactions - e.g. when the user double-clicks on a group changing items in the timeline - e.g. when an entity is highlighted To attach an event handler to an event, use the timeline.on() function. To detach it, use timeline.off() . When an event fires, any attached event handler functions are passed a single object containing all the details of the event, for example which buttons or EventModifierKeys were active during the event. Use destructuring to extract only the properties you need: To prevent default behavior of an event, call the preventDefault() function in the event handler: Some events, such as pointer-move or drag-move, fire continuously as the pointer moves. You can add a debouncing or throttling function to control this: Check out the Events story to see events in action. Sub-items # When a user interacts with some types of items on the timeline, an event will return a subItem object in its handler that contains more information about the sub-item. Nexus Timeline returns a sub-item object for Heatmap Cells and Data Points."},{"t":"Handling Events","g":"Handling Events","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/handling-events.html#handling-events"},{"t":"Sub-items","g":"Handling Events","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/handling-events.html#sub-items"},{"t":"Nexus Integration","g":"Timeline · Integration","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/graph-nexus-integration.html","x":"Nexus Integration Integration with Nexus # Nexus Timeline can be integrated with Nexus to show both timeline and graph visualizations of your data. You can configure your application so that when the data updates or a user interaction changes the timeline, the changes are reflected in the chart and vice versa. See the Integration section of our Storybook for examples of Nexus Timeline with Nexus. There's no one standard way to integrate Nexus Timeline with a chart. How you do it will depend on your data model and the interaction patterns you want to provide to your end users. Some points you might want to consider are: How to model and convert your data between the chart and the timeline. Will you map timeline events directly to the chart links? How to display the app components. Do you want to display the timeline and the chart side by side, or in separate tabs? Do you also want to include a time bar? What interaction patterns will you use? The examples below show two of the most common interaction patterns to get you started. Here we are showing the code for plain JavaScript. If you'd like to see the React version, toggle to the React page. Range and getInRangeItems # Keep the ch"},{"t":"Integration with Nexus","g":"Nexus Integration","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/graph-nexus-integration.html#integration"},{"t":"Range and getInRangeItems","g":"Nexus Integration","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/graph-nexus-integration.html#range-and-getinrangeitems"},{"t":"Focus and Selection","g":"Nexus Integration","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/graph-nexus-integration.html#focus-and-selection"},{"t":"Export","g":"Timeline · Integration","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/export.html","x":"Export You can export a static png image of your current timeline view using the export function. Try exporting a timeline in our Export story."},{"t":"Migration","g":"Timeline · Integration","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/migration.html","x":"Migration Nexus Timeline 2.0 changes the way data is displayed and how users interact with it. This guide gives an overview of these changes to help you upgrade and make the most of the new features. The key changes are: Nexus Timeline uses a new lens to visualize and examine large datasets. The concept of closed groups and any related API are removed. You should be able to load your data into Nexus Timeline 2.0 without issues. However, your data may look very different, especially if you have more entities than can fit on the screen at one time. You may want to think about how to communicate this to your end users. Introducing lens # In earlier versions, when the timeline contained more entities than could fit on the screen, Nexus Timeline collapsed entities into 'runs', or, if you've defined types or groups in your data, 'closed groups'. Both were opened using the expand control. Our users suggested that closed groups could sometimes open and close unpredictably when navigating the timeline, and that the design of narrow bands of data with lots of white space above and below could be improved. Nexus Timeline 2.0 draws all entities and their events, even if there isn't enough spac"},{"t":"Introducing lens","g":"Migration","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/migration.html#introducing-lens"},{"t":"Migration tips","g":"Migration","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/migration.html#migration-tips"},{"t":"Removed APIs","g":"Migration","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/migration.html#removed-apis"},{"t":"Other API changes","g":"Migration","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/migration.html#other-api-changes"},{"t":"Architecture","g":"Timeline · About","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/architecture.html","x":"Architecture Architecture # Security # Nexus Timeline is a low-risk, highly secure JavaScript library that is unlikely to be affected by common security vulnerabilities: No native data transfers with remote servers or server-side dependencies. No user data tracking or persistent data on local storage. Runs entirely within the browser using standard JavaScript. It has no plug-in or extension requirements. Source modules are readable ES modules; release bundles may be minified. Nothing is added to the global scope, protecting its functions. Nexus Timeline does not pollute prototypes. New APIs and features are tested to prevent introduction of vulnerabilities. Graph Nexus is implemented in dependency-free JavaScript ES modules and bundled with esbuild. The project tests its public APIs, rendering, and interactions before release. There is no accepted standard scanner for malicious JavaScript code. Our JavaScript files are built using secure processes and hosted on secure web servers. We will never add malicious behaviors to our source code, and we are confident that third parties cannot hijack or compromise our downloads. Dependencies # If you use the optional React wrapper, install R"},{"t":"Architecture","g":"Architecture","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/architecture.html#security"},{"t":"Dependencies","g":"Architecture","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/architecture.html#dependencies"},{"t":"TypeScript","g":"Architecture","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/architecture.html#typescript"},{"t":"Accessibility","g":"Timeline · About","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/accessibility.html","x":"Accessibility Accessibility # Designing accessible products means they can be used effectively by as many people as possible, providing an inclusive experience for people with impairments and different needs. Accessible design improves the product for everyone, as well as creating a better user experience for users with disabilities. In addition, most countries support accessibility with laws and regulations on both public and private sector organizations. There is no single universal standard, so we recommend that you always check what policies are relevant for you. However, the W3C's Web Content Accessibility Guidelines 2.1 are most often referenced as an acceptable standard. Compliance with Regulations # Nexus Timeline is not a complete application by itself, it is a toolkit that you integrate into your own product. This means that accessibility should be considered at the level of the end product to give your application users the best user experience possible. Nexus Timeline as a visual medium is often exempt from compliance requirements because the functionality cannot always be fully reproduced in an accessible way. To help you with your accessibility audits, we have prepare"},{"t":"Accessibility","g":"Accessibility","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/accessibility.html#accessibility"},{"t":"Compliance with Regulations","g":"Accessibility","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/accessibility.html#compliance"},{"t":"Keyboard Controls","g":"Accessibility","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/accessibility.html#keyboard-controls"},{"t":"Accessible Styling","g":"Accessibility","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/accessibility.html#accessible-styling"},{"t":"ARIA Support","g":"Accessibility","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/accessibility.html#aria-support"},{"t":"Obfuscation Requirements","g":"Timeline · About","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/obfuscation-requirements.html","x":"Obfuscation Requirements Graph Nexus ships readable ES-module source and does not require obfuscation, bundle-size padding, path renaming, branding removal, or expiry logic. Use your application's normal bundling, minification, and source-map policy."},{"t":"Alpha, Beta and Deprecated Features","g":"Timeline · About","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/alpha-beta-features.html","x":"Alpha, Beta and Deprecated Features Alpha features # Alpha features are marked in the API with an Alpha tag. This is a developmental stage, so we might introduce breaking changes to the API. These are always announced in the Release Notes. You shouldn't use alpha features in production. We don't provide customer support for alpha features used in a production environment, but we do support alpha features in a development environment. If you need any help with an alpha feature, please contact support. Beta features # Beta features are marked in the API with a Beta tag. Some features are introduced as beta from the start, while other alpha features move to beta after further development based on user feedback. This is still a developmental stage, so we might introduce breaking changes to the API. These are always announced in the Release Notes. You can deploy beta features to production, but note that breaking changes will affect your production upgrades. Once we've made all intended improvements to a beta feature, it moves out of beta and becomes a stable part of Nexus Timeline. If you need any help with a beta feature, please contact support. Deprecated features # Deprecated featur"},{"t":"Alpha features","g":"Alpha, Beta and Deprecated Features","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/alpha-beta-features.html#alpha-features"},{"t":"Beta features","g":"Alpha, Beta and Deprecated Features","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/alpha-beta-features.html#beta-features"},{"t":"Deprecated features","g":"Alpha, Beta and Deprecated Features","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/alpha-beta-features.html#deprecated-features"},{"t":"Breaking changes","g":"Alpha, Beta and Deprecated Features","u":"graph-nexus/timeline/js/alpha-beta-features.html#breaking-changes"}];
